WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:10.120
So I'm going to spend a few
minutes just finishing up

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:15.000
the lecture for Monday and
specifically as extreme recap

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.280
of what we're seeing.

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:22.120
We started to introduce the
learning based control setting,

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:25.685
whereby the underlying
assumption for all this,

00:00:25.685 --> 00:00:27.560
motivation for all of
this, is that we wanted

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:32.119
to relax our assumption that
we know exactly the system

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:33.380
that we're trying to control.

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:35.880
So we might have even unknown
dynamics or some parameters

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:37.320
that we wish to estimate.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:39.600
And so we started
essentially to introduce

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:42.240
on Monday kind of, if you
wish, the classical approaches

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:43.800
to learning based control.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:46.080
Where specifically, we
started looking first

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:50.040
at system identification
or sys ID,

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:52.080
where the idea of
system identification

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:56.280
is, broadly speaking, let's
collect a data set of rollouts

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:59.230
or experience in our system.

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:03.030
These might look different
trajectories that we have

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:05.650
while we experience the
evolution of our system.

00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:09.290
Let's take this data set, frame
this as a regression problem,

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:10.010
if you wish.

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:14.350
And let's try to approximate
the model of our dynamics.

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:17.310
We saw this through the
perspective of linear regression

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:19.870
in particular, but this
is a general concept

00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:24.470
where we want to approximate
some unknown dynamics from data.

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:27.230
We then introduced
another class of method,

00:01:27.230 --> 00:01:33.190
namely adaptive control,
where we looked specifically

00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:36.408
at MRAC or model reference
adaptive control,

00:01:36.408 --> 00:01:37.950
where the idea here
is that we wanted

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:46.470
to consider the coupled system
comprised of the controller.

00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:49.270
An adaptive component.

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:53.830
That basically is a mechanism
to basically adapt or adjust

00:01:53.830 --> 00:01:58.340
some maybe unknown parameters
that we wish to estimate.

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:00.040
And the system dynamics.

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:05.400
So we consider this
joint or coupled system.

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:07.820
And the overall goal
of adaptive control

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:10.360
is to study the
stability of the system.

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:13.660
And so we use Lyapunov
stability as our tool

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:16.500
of reference for measuring,
for estimating or analyzing

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:17.520
the stability.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:20.100
But this is, loosely
speaking, what we're doing.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:22.040
Model reference adaptive
control, we saw,

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:24.020
is the direct method
where we wanted

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:28.540
to use the adaptive component
specifically to directly improve

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:30.140
our controller.

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:32.380
And the term reference
relates to the fact

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:33.980
that we want to
adapt our controller

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:36.380
to track a reference signal.

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:38.860
And now, we're going to spend
maybe just a couple of slides

00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:41.700
on model identification
adaptive control, which

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:45.460
is another approach that
falls under the category

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:47.420
of adaptive control methods.

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:50.460
But before we get there, so
just a few clarifications

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:53.620
on model reference
adaptive control.

00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:56.840
Remember that we went through
a couple of examples which

00:02:56.840 --> 00:03:00.840
are, in some way, contrived from
the perspective of the analysis

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:04.200
and stability and
tools and analysis

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:06.880
from the perspective
of stability.

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:10.680
And we did this
in a mental model

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:13.680
was essentially
assuming that we had

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:16.840
a few things coming
in at different stages

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:17.700
of our analysis.

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:20.520
So we assume that
somebody was giving us

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:24.160
a smart way of defining
the control law u.

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:25.720
Someone was giving
us a smart way

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:29.400
of defining the Lyapunov
function v. The same thing

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:30.820
for the adaptive component.

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:33.960
And so our point here
was that in reality, we

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:36.500
would have to come up ourselves
with these components.

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:38.895
And then you do the
stability analysis.

00:03:38.895 --> 00:03:40.520
For the purpose of
this course, we just

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:43.400
wanted to focus on showing
the kind of analysis that

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:46.880
would come from model
reference adaptive control.

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:49.800
And so the first
reference is a good one

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:52.100
if you wish to see
the forward direction.

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:55.350
So deriving these things.

00:03:55.350 --> 00:03:58.350
A couple of notes on the
reference signal itself.

00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:02.730
So we introduced this
reference signal as, again,

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:06.210
something that came from outside
for the purpose of our analysis.

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:08.190
But in general, if
the reference signal

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:14.190
is very simple, such as either
0 or a constant throughout time.

00:04:14.190 --> 00:04:18.510
Then it is possible that we
might have many different values

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:20.709
of our parameters that
we wish to estimate

00:04:20.709 --> 00:04:24.090
that enable us to perfectly
track this reference signal.

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:30.390
And so in a way, this comes
with an identifiability issue

00:04:30.390 --> 00:04:32.270
with respect to the
actual parameters

00:04:32.270 --> 00:04:33.690
that we wish to estimate.

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:36.670
Because again, here
in MRAC, we typically

00:04:36.670 --> 00:04:39.350
only care about the overall
performance with respect

00:04:39.350 --> 00:04:40.410
to reference tracking.

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:42.110
And so we don't really
care necessarily

00:04:42.110 --> 00:04:44.950
about parameter estimation.

00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:49.070
On the other hand, if the signal
is too complex or at least

00:04:49.070 --> 00:04:51.940
complex enough such that the
only way to actually track

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:55.860
it is to estimate the true value
of those unknown parameters,

00:04:55.860 --> 00:05:00.220
then this creates a problem in
the sense that we, or at least,

00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:03.260
it creates a tension towards
caring about parameter

00:05:03.260 --> 00:05:05.020
estimation, because we.

00:05:05.020 --> 00:05:07.740
And so this kind of brings
up again all our discussions

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:10.340
that we did on persistent
excitation in the sense

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:12.760
that if we wish to estimate
the true parameters,

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:16.580
then we might want to probe
the system in meaningful ways

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:19.200
in order to converge
to the true parameters.

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:26.060
Any questions on this general
recap and on the overall picture

00:05:26.060 --> 00:05:27.720
here?

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:28.220
Yes.

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:30.100
[INAUDIBLE]

00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:31.200
Yeah, indeed.

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:34.460
It's a good point.

00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:36.700
Let's see.

00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:38.820
Well, now these ones--

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:40.700
let's quickly mention
a couple of things

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:44.460
on model identification
adaptive control.

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:46.700
Specifically, I
mean, the discussion

00:05:46.700 --> 00:05:49.180
on model identification
is very case-dependent

00:05:49.180 --> 00:05:52.970
because at the high level,
what MIAC is doing is basically

00:05:52.970 --> 00:05:54.590
using system identification.

00:05:54.590 --> 00:05:56.410
So estimating a
model of aerodynamics

00:05:56.410 --> 00:05:58.730
as an inner loop for control.

00:05:58.730 --> 00:06:00.970
So what we do is
that MIAC essentially

00:06:00.970 --> 00:06:03.930
combines model estimation
with a controller that

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:08.330
uses this estimated model
as if it was the true one.

00:06:08.330 --> 00:06:11.330
So in a way, this is
extremely customizable

00:06:11.330 --> 00:06:13.498
because you could
pick your own model

00:06:13.498 --> 00:06:14.790
that you're trying to estimate.

00:06:14.790 --> 00:06:18.350
You could pick your own
controller that uses this model.

00:06:18.350 --> 00:06:20.430
And the two things are
fairly independent,

00:06:20.430 --> 00:06:22.330
the one from the other.

00:06:22.330 --> 00:06:24.250
One distinction that
typically differentiates

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:27.050
between different
methods is whether we

00:06:27.050 --> 00:06:30.930
are using working under
uncertainty equivalent

00:06:30.930 --> 00:06:31.970
assumption.

00:06:31.970 --> 00:06:35.670
That is we are, for the
purpose of modeling,

00:06:35.670 --> 00:06:41.810
we assume that we are estimating
some parameters theta.

00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:45.810
And these parameters theta
are obviously the goal

00:06:45.810 --> 00:06:47.590
of our estimation process.

00:06:47.590 --> 00:06:51.830
And so there is uncertainty on
our current estimate of theta.

00:06:51.830 --> 00:06:53.350
And so in the
certainty equivalent,

00:06:53.350 --> 00:06:57.390
what we're typically assuming
is that our guess, so theta hat,

00:06:57.390 --> 00:06:58.690
is a point estimate.

00:06:58.690 --> 00:07:01.290
So we are currently
estimating one value of theta,

00:07:01.290 --> 00:07:02.870
and we trust it fully.

00:07:02.870 --> 00:07:05.510
On the other hand, in
the cautious approach,

00:07:05.510 --> 00:07:07.990
we are trying to
estimate a distribution

00:07:07.990 --> 00:07:10.510
over the unknown
parameters theta, where

00:07:10.510 --> 00:07:12.350
the distribution
essentially is a mechanism

00:07:12.350 --> 00:07:16.230
to express our uncertainty
on the estimate itself.

00:07:16.230 --> 00:07:18.190
So we have some data
that maybe leads us

00:07:18.190 --> 00:07:21.150
into thinking that
theta hat is the more

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:25.210
plausible value for theta, but
we have some uncertainty on it.

00:07:25.210 --> 00:07:28.070
And so in the cautious
approach, we're

00:07:28.070 --> 00:07:32.510
basically trying to maintain
this metric, this estimate

00:07:32.510 --> 00:07:37.530
of uncertainty and keep this
also potentially for control.

00:07:41.070 --> 00:07:44.470
So at a high level,
kind of MRAC and MIAC

00:07:44.470 --> 00:07:46.620
kind of arise from two
different perspectives.

00:07:46.620 --> 00:07:49.820
So in MRAC, the parameters
are updated directly

00:07:49.820 --> 00:07:54.740
to minimize tracking error with
respect to our reference signal.

00:07:54.740 --> 00:07:56.980
On the other hand,
in MIAC, parameters

00:07:56.980 --> 00:08:00.120
are updated, such as to
minimize data fitting error.

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:02.660
So we have this intermediate
objective, which

00:08:02.660 --> 00:08:06.100
is one of parameter estimation.

00:08:06.100 --> 00:08:08.035
And as we were saying,
MIAC controllers

00:08:08.035 --> 00:08:09.660
are generally more
flexible because one

00:08:09.660 --> 00:08:13.460
can couple different models
and controllers more easily.

00:08:13.460 --> 00:08:16.740
But at the same time,
MIAC controllers typically

00:08:16.740 --> 00:08:19.060
rely on having a
good estimate of what

00:08:19.060 --> 00:08:21.500
the unknown parameters are.

00:08:21.500 --> 00:08:24.100
And guaranteeing the
performance and stability

00:08:24.100 --> 00:08:28.300
of MIAC controllers is typically
harder because of this reliance

00:08:28.300 --> 00:08:32.179
on having to estimate
the true parameters.

00:08:32.179 --> 00:08:35.659
On the other hand, for MRAC
stability and convergence

00:08:35.659 --> 00:08:38.799
are usually achieved
by definition.

00:08:38.799 --> 00:08:40.880
Because we are designing
the full system

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:42.559
such as to guarantee stability.

00:08:46.570 --> 00:08:50.330
And so this
concludes the content

00:08:50.330 --> 00:08:55.150
that I wanted to discuss
about adaptive control.

00:08:55.150 --> 00:08:57.990
And so we switch into the
content for today's lecture,

00:08:57.990 --> 00:09:00.010
which is basically an
introduction to imitation

00:09:00.010 --> 00:09:03.930
learning and
reinforcement learning.

00:09:03.930 --> 00:09:06.370
And from the perspective
of our usual roadmap,

00:09:06.370 --> 00:09:11.410
we are basically moving from
being in the top left quadrant

00:09:11.410 --> 00:09:12.930
that is more
classical approaches

00:09:12.930 --> 00:09:16.130
to learning-based control
to the top right, where

00:09:16.130 --> 00:09:19.250
we are essentially starting
to introduce what I think

00:09:19.250 --> 00:09:22.250
is probably one of the most
active areas of research

00:09:22.250 --> 00:09:27.250
right now when looking at
control and autonomous systems.

00:09:27.250 --> 00:09:30.970
Namely because there has
been a push towards end

00:09:30.970 --> 00:09:33.730
to end approaches and
learning-based methods

00:09:33.730 --> 00:09:37.050
to allow robots and autonomous
systems to be deployed

00:09:37.050 --> 00:09:39.330
in practice.

00:09:39.330 --> 00:09:44.160
And so in this sense,
you might have stumbled,

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:46.712
and you might have seen a
lot of different videos,

00:09:46.712 --> 00:09:48.920
and you might have been
following the recent progress

00:09:48.920 --> 00:09:51.720
that is happening in robotics.

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:55.440
There are different reasons
for why this progress is

00:09:55.440 --> 00:09:56.960
being happening.

00:09:56.960 --> 00:10:01.120
One of this is obviously
the strong progress

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:03.380
from the perspective
of foundation models,

00:10:03.380 --> 00:10:05.800
leveraging large language
models or vision language models

00:10:05.800 --> 00:10:06.920
for control.

00:10:06.920 --> 00:10:10.200
But at the high level,
all of these different end

00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:12.880
to end pipelines that are
coming up over the last three

00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:15.380
to four years are really,
at the end of the day,

00:10:15.380 --> 00:10:17.600
being learned through
either imitation learning

00:10:17.600 --> 00:10:18.700
or reinforcement learning.

00:10:18.700 --> 00:10:21.160
So these are the ground
truth learning signals

00:10:21.160 --> 00:10:25.600
that all of these methods and
architectures are leveraging.

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:27.720
And of course, their
impact is not only

00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:31.220
for the purpose of
robot manipulation.

00:10:31.220 --> 00:10:34.200
They are being used a lot
also in autonomous driving.

00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:39.120
So you can see here on the left,
this is pretty recent autonomous

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:41.540
stack from NVIDIA,
the Alpamayo, which

00:10:41.540 --> 00:10:44.440
is using indeed both
imitation learning,

00:10:44.440 --> 00:10:46.940
so supervised learning,
and reinforcement learning

00:10:46.940 --> 00:10:49.220
to train part of the stack.

00:10:49.220 --> 00:10:53.340
And also, these methods,
namely reinforcement learning,

00:10:53.340 --> 00:10:56.460
are used for the alignment
of large language

00:10:56.460 --> 00:10:58.060
models and visual
language models

00:10:58.060 --> 00:11:00.700
nowadays, where the
idea here is that we

00:11:00.700 --> 00:11:04.780
want to take these large
neural networks that represent

00:11:04.780 --> 00:11:07.660
large language models and
fine tuned them to be useful

00:11:07.660 --> 00:11:08.580
assistants.

00:11:08.580 --> 00:11:12.760
And so typically, this alignment
phase happens in different ways,

00:11:12.760 --> 00:11:14.980
but the main bulk of
the work is typically

00:11:14.980 --> 00:11:18.860
done through reinforcement
learning in current stacks.

00:11:18.860 --> 00:11:21.780
So all of this to say
is that these mechanisms

00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:28.420
are highly relevant in
current industry and research.

00:11:28.420 --> 00:11:32.060
And therefore, I think they
are extremely exciting.

00:11:32.060 --> 00:11:34.260
But for the purpose
of our class,

00:11:34.260 --> 00:11:37.180
what is the usual, what
is the setting that we

00:11:37.180 --> 00:11:38.340
are interested in?

00:11:38.340 --> 00:11:41.090
And specifically, I would
say that basically all

00:11:41.090 --> 00:11:43.930
of the methods that we have
been focusing on so far

00:11:43.930 --> 00:11:49.050
are essentially trying to
define a mechanism from which

00:11:49.050 --> 00:11:52.370
we can go from some
representation of the state,

00:11:52.370 --> 00:11:55.130
which could be more
or less structured,

00:11:55.130 --> 00:11:59.970
to the definition of
an optimal control.

00:11:59.970 --> 00:12:01.650
So this is basically
the gold standard

00:12:01.650 --> 00:12:03.610
that we're aiming
for whenever we

00:12:03.610 --> 00:12:08.130
are devising all of the methods
that we looked at in this class.

00:12:08.130 --> 00:12:10.627
And so far, I would
say most of the methods

00:12:10.627 --> 00:12:12.210
or actually all of
the methods that we

00:12:12.210 --> 00:12:15.650
have been looking at fall under
this option one category, which

00:12:15.650 --> 00:12:20.370
is basically entails
having to acquire

00:12:20.370 --> 00:12:22.510
a deep understanding
of the problem setting,

00:12:22.510 --> 00:12:27.610
for example, in the form of an
equation of the system dynamics,

00:12:27.610 --> 00:12:29.970
and then design a
solution that leverages

00:12:29.970 --> 00:12:31.950
this deep understanding
of our system.

00:12:31.950 --> 00:12:34.337
You can think of
indirect methods

00:12:34.337 --> 00:12:36.170
that we looked at the
beginning of the class

00:12:36.170 --> 00:12:39.220
as the extreme canonical
example of this,

00:12:39.220 --> 00:12:41.840
where we used our
understanding of the system

00:12:41.840 --> 00:12:46.600
to derive optimality
conditions for control.

00:12:46.600 --> 00:12:50.320
On the other hand, what the
tools we'll start to see today

00:12:50.320 --> 00:12:52.760
and for the rest of
this class belong

00:12:52.760 --> 00:12:57.240
to either option two or option
three here on this slide,

00:12:57.240 --> 00:13:00.840
where at a high level,
we want to set basically

00:13:00.840 --> 00:13:03.520
this problem of going from
a state representation

00:13:03.520 --> 00:13:06.400
to an action as a
learning problem.

00:13:06.400 --> 00:13:08.020
And we'll be doing
this in two ways.

00:13:08.020 --> 00:13:10.560
So one way will be through
imitation learning,

00:13:10.560 --> 00:13:15.200
where the idea is
that in some way,

00:13:15.200 --> 00:13:17.140
or as the quote is
saying here on the slide,

00:13:17.140 --> 00:13:19.760
we want to learn
from demonstrations.

00:13:19.760 --> 00:13:22.320
And the idea will be that
we will be collecting

00:13:22.320 --> 00:13:26.160
data sets of experience, which
are then represented as if you

00:13:26.160 --> 00:13:29.000
want tuples of
states and decisions

00:13:29.000 --> 00:13:31.160
that were taken in
those states, and we

00:13:31.160 --> 00:13:35.670
want to try to estimate and
approximate this mapping.

00:13:35.670 --> 00:13:37.710
So in this case,
the demonstrations

00:13:37.710 --> 00:13:40.430
are essentially
relating to the fact

00:13:40.430 --> 00:13:44.030
that we have an example of what
is hopefully a good behavior

00:13:44.030 --> 00:13:48.433
to take in a specific state.

00:13:48.433 --> 00:13:50.850
For the case of reinforcement
learning, on the other hand,

00:13:50.850 --> 00:13:53.010
we want to learn
by trial and error.

00:13:53.010 --> 00:13:56.110
That is we'll be learning
from data that is basically

00:13:56.110 --> 00:14:01.390
trying to pair states of we
find ourselves in with a score

00:14:01.390 --> 00:14:03.190
or metric that
basically tells us

00:14:03.190 --> 00:14:06.830
how good a specific action
or control is in that state.

00:14:06.830 --> 00:14:08.750
So I hope that
you're seeing already

00:14:08.750 --> 00:14:12.252
the kind of philosophical
distinction between these two

00:14:12.252 --> 00:14:13.710
approaches, because
on one hand, we

00:14:13.710 --> 00:14:17.273
have a teacher that tells us
what is a good action to take.

00:14:17.273 --> 00:14:19.690
On the other hand, for the
case of reinforcement learning,

00:14:19.690 --> 00:14:22.790
we don't have an entity that
tells us what is a good behavior

00:14:22.790 --> 00:14:25.310
to take, but we have a
measure that tells us

00:14:25.310 --> 00:14:27.510
how good a specific action is.

00:14:27.510 --> 00:14:29.630
And so the overall goal
of reinforcement learning

00:14:29.630 --> 00:14:32.190
will be to try out
different strategies

00:14:32.190 --> 00:14:36.110
and figure out which one is
the most rewarding in practice.

00:14:36.110 --> 00:14:36.610
Yes.

00:14:36.610 --> 00:14:38.330
[INAUDIBLE]

00:14:44.930 --> 00:14:49.570
Is the data that you're feeding
into your algorithm always going

00:14:49.570 --> 00:14:52.190
to be treated as
the perfect model,

00:14:52.190 --> 00:14:55.050
or is there a way to
inform the algorithm you're

00:14:55.050 --> 00:14:58.470
working with that this is an
example of something good,

00:14:58.470 --> 00:15:00.700
but it can be done better?

00:15:00.700 --> 00:15:01.950
Yeah, this is a good question.

00:15:01.950 --> 00:15:03.970
And I think it
touches on, I would

00:15:03.970 --> 00:15:08.210
say one of the main limitations,
if you wish, of imitation

00:15:08.210 --> 00:15:08.930
learning.

00:15:08.930 --> 00:15:11.450
Because at face value,
imitation learning

00:15:11.450 --> 00:15:13.930
assumes that the
mapping that you're

00:15:13.930 --> 00:15:15.810
trying to approximate
from your data

00:15:15.810 --> 00:15:18.930
is actually representative
of good solutions.

00:15:18.930 --> 00:15:21.330
So here, in a way,
imitation learning

00:15:21.330 --> 00:15:23.810
is kind of bounded by the
performance of the behaviors

00:15:23.810 --> 00:15:27.130
that you have in your data.

00:15:27.130 --> 00:15:30.970
This is a statement that has an
asterisk in a way because there

00:15:30.970 --> 00:15:32.920
are different ways
of instantiating

00:15:32.920 --> 00:15:37.880
the imitation learning problem
to maybe favor high performance

00:15:37.880 --> 00:15:41.080
behavior with respect to
others in the data set.

00:15:41.080 --> 00:15:42.520
But in general,
this is the case,

00:15:42.520 --> 00:15:45.560
where your performance that
you can get out of your data

00:15:45.560 --> 00:15:48.620
is typically bottlenecked by
the performance of your teacher,

00:15:48.620 --> 00:15:51.080
if that makes sense.

00:15:51.080 --> 00:15:53.720
Yes.

00:15:53.720 --> 00:15:57.100
[INAUDIBLE] machine learning
to learn the basics,

00:15:57.100 --> 00:15:58.760
I guess, and then
move to reinforcement

00:15:58.760 --> 00:16:00.888
learning to tune it
more effectively, where

00:16:00.888 --> 00:16:02.680
it's easier to make it
[INAUDIBLE] once you

00:16:02.680 --> 00:16:03.960
know your task.

00:16:03.960 --> 00:16:04.480
Yeah.

00:16:04.480 --> 00:16:06.022
So this is one of
the points we'll be

00:16:06.022 --> 00:16:07.920
doing in a few slides from now.

00:16:07.920 --> 00:16:12.360
But the idea is that I would
say current autonomy stacks

00:16:12.360 --> 00:16:15.040
and the training of
current autonomy stacks

00:16:15.040 --> 00:16:17.120
is typically a layered
process, where you have

00:16:17.120 --> 00:16:18.920
different phases of training.

00:16:18.920 --> 00:16:21.320
I would say this has
been inherited mostly

00:16:21.320 --> 00:16:24.880
by the large language model
community and the NLP community,

00:16:24.880 --> 00:16:28.117
where these things, these large
models have stages of training.

00:16:28.117 --> 00:16:29.950
So you have maybe a
vast pre-training stage,

00:16:29.950 --> 00:16:34.870
where you're just learning
the system and general rules

00:16:34.870 --> 00:16:36.630
of the world, and
then you're trying

00:16:36.630 --> 00:16:40.070
to distill more and more high
quality data along the way.

00:16:40.070 --> 00:16:42.485
And so usually, yes, I think
you have a good intuition,

00:16:42.485 --> 00:16:44.110
where supervised
learning and imitation

00:16:44.110 --> 00:16:46.850
learning could be a way to
get a good starting point.

00:16:46.850 --> 00:16:49.490
And then reinforcement learning
could be used to fine tune.

00:16:49.490 --> 00:16:49.990
Yeah.

00:16:54.230 --> 00:16:54.730
OK.

00:16:54.730 --> 00:16:56.990
And so in this context,
what we'll be doing today

00:16:56.990 --> 00:17:00.750
is we'll start by just
fixing kind of our notation

00:17:00.750 --> 00:17:02.650
and understanding
unsupervised learning.

00:17:02.650 --> 00:17:06.750
And then we'll go into a
discussion on imitation learning

00:17:06.750 --> 00:17:09.670
and reinforcement learning,
where the idea for today

00:17:09.670 --> 00:17:13.970
is to basically fixate and
understand, I would say,

00:17:13.970 --> 00:17:16.109
the fundamentals of
what imitation learning

00:17:16.109 --> 00:17:17.990
and reinforcement learning are.

00:17:17.990 --> 00:17:20.829
In general, the distinction and
similarities between these two

00:17:20.829 --> 00:17:23.190
approaches and how they
relate with each other.

00:17:23.190 --> 00:17:25.756
So I would say at
a high level, I

00:17:25.756 --> 00:17:27.339
think that you should
see this lecture

00:17:27.339 --> 00:17:30.060
as trying to build a
mental model of how

00:17:30.060 --> 00:17:34.562
these different tools are
organized and should be seen.

00:17:34.562 --> 00:17:36.520
And in the following
weeks, starting on Monday,

00:17:36.520 --> 00:17:38.460
we'll then start to
actually introduce

00:17:38.460 --> 00:17:42.080
concretely different methods
that belong to these families.

00:17:46.325 --> 00:17:47.700
And of course, as
I said, this is

00:17:47.700 --> 00:17:51.080
one of the most active
areas of research right now.

00:17:51.080 --> 00:17:56.780
So there are plenty of
great references out there.

00:17:56.780 --> 00:18:00.220
Apart from the textbook,
from the class definitely.

00:18:00.220 --> 00:18:02.340
The Sutton and Barto for
reinforcement learning

00:18:02.340 --> 00:18:03.977
is a great reference.

00:18:03.977 --> 00:18:06.560
And there are also a number of
classes, both here at Stanford,

00:18:06.560 --> 00:18:10.380
for example, Deep RL, and
the kind of equivalent on it

00:18:10.380 --> 00:18:13.340
at Berkeley.

00:18:13.340 --> 00:18:16.000
But so starting from
supervised learning,

00:18:16.000 --> 00:18:17.860
I would say in
general, supervised

00:18:17.860 --> 00:18:20.220
learning describe
these tasks where

00:18:20.220 --> 00:18:24.340
we want to learn a mapping,
here denoted as f on the slides,

00:18:24.340 --> 00:18:29.610
from some input set
x to an output set y.

00:18:29.610 --> 00:18:32.530
And this represents
a general formalism

00:18:32.530 --> 00:18:34.730
where most regression
and, for example,

00:18:34.730 --> 00:18:38.410
classification tasks
can be understood.

00:18:38.410 --> 00:18:43.050
For example, we might want to
learn an image classifier, so

00:18:43.050 --> 00:18:48.090
something that maps from images
of animals or different scenes

00:18:48.090 --> 00:18:51.130
to a class category
of the object that

00:18:51.130 --> 00:18:52.570
is present in that scene.

00:18:52.570 --> 00:18:54.250
And so here in this
case, the mapping

00:18:54.250 --> 00:18:56.690
would essentially be learning
in this function that

00:18:56.690 --> 00:18:58.530
takes an input x,
here represented,

00:18:58.530 --> 00:19:02.130
for example, as an RGB
image, to the class that

00:19:02.130 --> 00:19:04.810
represents the object
present in that image.

00:19:04.810 --> 00:19:06.770
But more generally,
this is essentially

00:19:06.770 --> 00:19:08.650
the problem of
learning a mapping that

00:19:08.650 --> 00:19:10.830
goes from some input
representation,

00:19:10.830 --> 00:19:14.710
denoted usually as a vector x,
to an output representation,

00:19:14.710 --> 00:19:18.530
which could also
be under vector y.

00:19:18.530 --> 00:19:20.570
And when we say
learning, this typically

00:19:20.570 --> 00:19:24.990
involves data in the form of
input-output pairs, so xy,

00:19:24.990 --> 00:19:27.750
and we want to
approximate this mapping

00:19:27.750 --> 00:19:31.590
by typically kind of
methods along the lines

00:19:31.590 --> 00:19:34.130
of maximum likelihood
estimation.

00:19:39.710 --> 00:19:43.390
And to really set
down the notation

00:19:43.390 --> 00:19:45.750
we'll be using going
forward, let us go quickly

00:19:45.750 --> 00:19:47.330
through a simple example.

00:19:47.330 --> 00:19:49.550
So consider this
simple regression,

00:19:49.550 --> 00:19:55.270
where we have some input data
x and some output data y.

00:19:55.270 --> 00:19:58.350
And we have some examples and
examples of these mappings,

00:19:58.350 --> 00:20:01.430
represented by the red
dots here on the slide.

00:20:01.430 --> 00:20:04.990
Our problem in this case,
supervised learning problem,

00:20:04.990 --> 00:20:07.590
might be essentially
trying to fit

00:20:07.590 --> 00:20:11.350
a candidate kind of parabolic
function to this data.

00:20:11.350 --> 00:20:13.710
And so in this case, our
mapping that we wish to learn

00:20:13.710 --> 00:20:17.092
will be a parametric function f
of theta with parameters theta,

00:20:17.092 --> 00:20:18.550
and the supervised
learning problem

00:20:18.550 --> 00:20:21.190
will be to learn these
parameters theta that

00:20:21.190 --> 00:20:23.780
minimize some notion of error.

00:20:23.780 --> 00:20:25.940
And so we saw this
last time already

00:20:25.940 --> 00:20:29.100
could be, for example,
in the form of minimizing

00:20:29.100 --> 00:20:32.420
some squared error loss.

00:20:32.420 --> 00:20:33.980
Of course,
equivalently, this can

00:20:33.980 --> 00:20:37.380
be written from the perspective
of maximizing likelihood.

00:20:37.380 --> 00:20:40.820
So instead of seeing this
as an error minimization,

00:20:40.820 --> 00:20:43.100
when we want to
minimize the discrepancy

00:20:43.100 --> 00:20:46.260
between our predictions
and the actual realizations

00:20:46.260 --> 00:20:51.220
of some mapping that we wish
to estimate, we might be posing

00:20:51.220 --> 00:20:56.060
this as a probabilistic learning
problem, where our function,

00:20:56.060 --> 00:20:59.340
we basically hypothesize
that our data is distributed

00:20:59.340 --> 00:21:01.440
according to some distribution.

00:21:01.440 --> 00:21:04.700
For example, here, the
probability of a point

00:21:04.700 --> 00:21:09.260
y given x might be represented
by a Gaussian distribution,

00:21:09.260 --> 00:21:11.580
where our function
could represent

00:21:11.580 --> 00:21:13.620
the mean of this
Gaussian distribution

00:21:13.620 --> 00:21:16.700
given some known variance.

00:21:16.700 --> 00:21:19.500
So pictorially here is
as if we were placing

00:21:19.500 --> 00:21:22.130
some small Gaussians
on top of each point,

00:21:22.130 --> 00:21:24.250
and the overall
learning problem will

00:21:24.250 --> 00:21:28.770
be to maximize the joint
probability of all our data

00:21:28.770 --> 00:21:29.350
points.

00:21:32.370 --> 00:21:36.290
And so learning through maximum
through likelihood maximization

00:21:36.290 --> 00:21:38.170
can be written as
basically taking

00:21:38.170 --> 00:21:43.130
the argmax of the product of
all the conditional probability

00:21:43.130 --> 00:21:46.250
distributions of our
observed data yi's

00:21:46.250 --> 00:21:50.770
given our prediction f of xi.

00:21:50.770 --> 00:21:53.370
Mathematically, this
is typically not

00:21:53.370 --> 00:21:55.810
very well-behaved
object because we

00:21:55.810 --> 00:22:00.410
have the product of a bunch of
terms that are between 0 and 1.

00:22:00.410 --> 00:22:02.050
And so what people
do in practice

00:22:02.050 --> 00:22:04.970
is that rather than
maximizing the likelihood,

00:22:04.970 --> 00:22:08.430
we would tend to minimize
the negative log likelihood.

00:22:08.430 --> 00:22:10.730
So we are taking the
logarithm of this,

00:22:10.730 --> 00:22:14.590
and The logarithm is a
monotonic transformation.

00:22:14.590 --> 00:22:17.910
So it basically preserves
the extremum points.

00:22:17.910 --> 00:22:20.240
So the maximum are preserved.

00:22:20.240 --> 00:22:22.960
So in practice, you
would go from a situation

00:22:22.960 --> 00:22:28.520
where maybe your maximum
likelihood point would be

00:22:28.520 --> 00:22:30.385
with a very narrow or peaked.

00:22:30.385 --> 00:22:31.760
So this is the
objective function

00:22:31.760 --> 00:22:35.200
that we wish to maximize.

00:22:35.200 --> 00:22:39.620
So this would be maybe the
probability of a given x.

00:22:39.620 --> 00:22:43.600
If we take the log of
this, this would typically

00:22:43.600 --> 00:22:51.200
boil down to basically smoothing
out this loss function landscape

00:22:51.200 --> 00:22:53.500
while preserving the same point.

00:22:53.500 --> 00:22:57.500
So this might be the
same mu hat, for example.

00:23:02.960 --> 00:23:07.320
But I hope this is mostly
repetition and background

00:23:07.320 --> 00:23:11.000
for most of you guys.

00:23:11.000 --> 00:23:15.140
But why do we care about
supervised learning?

00:23:15.140 --> 00:23:17.880
And the point is that supervised
learning, as we will see,

00:23:17.880 --> 00:23:21.260
is basically the cornerstone
of imitation learning.

00:23:21.260 --> 00:23:23.780
And this is because imitation
learning essentially

00:23:23.780 --> 00:23:27.340
refers to a class
of methods that

00:23:27.340 --> 00:23:33.100
enable skills to be transferred
from an expert to a learner.

00:23:33.100 --> 00:23:35.540
And in the context of
robotics, this expert

00:23:35.540 --> 00:23:40.500
is typically a human operator
or some pre-existing control

00:23:40.500 --> 00:23:41.620
policy.

00:23:41.620 --> 00:23:43.980
And the learner is
basically our policy

00:23:43.980 --> 00:23:46.660
that we wish to approximate.

00:23:46.660 --> 00:23:50.820
So in this case, you could
think as basically, we

00:23:50.820 --> 00:23:55.860
have this bimanual
manipulation system where

00:23:55.860 --> 00:23:59.680
we have a human teleoperator
that is controlling this robot.

00:23:59.680 --> 00:24:04.780
And so for us, the human
kind of logging these actions

00:24:04.780 --> 00:24:10.660
will be the teacher
or the skilled expert.

00:24:10.660 --> 00:24:13.780
We would then be collecting
these data sets of controls

00:24:13.780 --> 00:24:15.270
that the human is taking.

00:24:15.270 --> 00:24:19.090
And then the learning problem
will then be basically

00:24:19.090 --> 00:24:21.890
figuring out how to
map from the states

00:24:21.890 --> 00:24:26.710
of this bimanual
manipulator to the controls.

00:24:26.710 --> 00:24:28.550
So basically
minimizing, for example,

00:24:28.550 --> 00:24:30.890
the error with respect
to what the humans would

00:24:30.890 --> 00:24:32.110
have done in that case.

00:24:38.210 --> 00:24:41.370
And so imitation
learning at a high level

00:24:41.370 --> 00:24:44.930
assumes access to a data set,
as we were saying before,

00:24:44.930 --> 00:24:49.890
of essentially pairs of
states x and controls u, that

00:24:49.890 --> 00:24:53.730
are collected by
observing some expert that

00:24:53.730 --> 00:24:56.170
is behaving in the world.

00:24:56.170 --> 00:24:59.690
And imitation learning should
be interpreted as an umbrella

00:24:59.690 --> 00:25:04.250
term that, broadly
speaking, comprehends

00:25:04.250 --> 00:25:06.390
two families of approaches.

00:25:06.390 --> 00:25:08.430
The first one is
behavior cloning.

00:25:08.430 --> 00:25:11.210
That is in a way also the
most intuitive of the two.

00:25:11.210 --> 00:25:14.240
Whereby in behavior cloning,
what we want to learn,

00:25:14.240 --> 00:25:16.520
or at least the goal
of learning is directly

00:25:16.520 --> 00:25:19.960
learning this policy that
maps from states to controls.

00:25:19.960 --> 00:25:23.760
So in behavior cloning,
our objective of learning

00:25:23.760 --> 00:25:27.480
will be to learn a parametric
representation of our policy pi

00:25:27.480 --> 00:25:30.480
that basically minimizes
some notion of error

00:25:30.480 --> 00:25:34.860
between the true x and new pairs
that we have in our data set

00:25:34.860 --> 00:25:38.320
and the one we are predicting.

00:25:38.320 --> 00:25:42.080
The other class of methods is
known as reinforcement learning

00:25:42.080 --> 00:25:45.600
or also often known as
inverse optimal control, where

00:25:45.600 --> 00:25:48.880
on the other hand,
the objective here

00:25:48.880 --> 00:25:55.000
is to approximate the reward
or objective that the expert is

00:25:55.000 --> 00:25:56.680
currently optimizing.

00:25:56.680 --> 00:26:00.000
So in some way, the
main goal or output

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:02.360
of an inverse reinforcement
learning algorithm

00:26:02.360 --> 00:26:06.440
will be to estimate reward
function r that maps

00:26:06.440 --> 00:26:10.120
pairs of states and controls
to a measure of success

00:26:10.120 --> 00:26:11.210
or performance.

00:26:14.670 --> 00:26:18.230
So you see how these two
families and categories

00:26:18.230 --> 00:26:19.990
of approaches are
in a way, nicely

00:26:19.990 --> 00:26:22.710
complementary to each other
because here, we're directly

00:26:22.710 --> 00:26:24.830
approximating the
mapping that leads us

00:26:24.830 --> 00:26:27.710
from states to controls.

00:26:27.710 --> 00:26:30.630
While here, we're approximating
the kind of objective

00:26:30.630 --> 00:26:34.270
that we think the expert
is optimizing and then use

00:26:34.270 --> 00:26:35.990
this for control.

00:26:35.990 --> 00:26:36.930
Yes.

00:26:36.930 --> 00:26:42.730
[INAUDIBLE] a policy,
like for example,

00:26:42.730 --> 00:26:45.310
if I'm picking up
something like back

00:26:45.310 --> 00:26:47.950
out the policy for what
I'm thinking kind of.

00:26:47.950 --> 00:26:48.730
Yeah, exactly.

00:26:48.730 --> 00:26:52.590
So it would be as if
maybe, I don't know,

00:26:52.590 --> 00:26:57.890
an example could be
in a navigation task.

00:26:57.890 --> 00:27:03.930
So say we have some task where
we have a starting point here

00:27:03.930 --> 00:27:06.750
and like a goal point here.

00:27:06.750 --> 00:27:08.750
In the behavior
cloning route, we

00:27:08.750 --> 00:27:11.650
would be collecting
examples of trajectories

00:27:11.650 --> 00:27:14.530
of how to get to the goal.

00:27:14.530 --> 00:27:16.930
And we would be imitating those.

00:27:16.930 --> 00:27:19.490
In the case of inverse
reinforcement learning, what

00:27:19.490 --> 00:27:22.310
we might be interested in
doing, on the other hand,

00:27:22.310 --> 00:27:24.650
is learning maybe a
sort of vector field,

00:27:24.650 --> 00:27:26.770
where the cost is
basically lower

00:27:26.770 --> 00:27:30.690
at the goal state and
increases in other states.

00:27:30.690 --> 00:27:32.850
So it's basically
something that tells us

00:27:32.850 --> 00:27:35.290
how to behave in that
sense from the perspective

00:27:35.290 --> 00:27:36.930
of the util operation, yes.

00:27:36.930 --> 00:27:40.370
This is what you were saying.

00:27:40.370 --> 00:27:42.160
Yeah.

00:27:42.160 --> 00:27:44.270
[INAUDIBLE] inverse
reinforcement learning,

00:27:44.270 --> 00:27:47.170
you still have to do the
optimization to find the best

00:27:47.170 --> 00:27:49.850
control given your
current state, like some--

00:27:49.850 --> 00:27:50.870
Yes, indeed.

00:27:50.870 --> 00:27:52.730
So at the end of
the day, this is not

00:27:52.730 --> 00:27:56.328
something that practically leads
to a rule of how to behave.

00:27:56.328 --> 00:27:57.870
So there would be
an additional step,

00:27:57.870 --> 00:27:59.690
which is having to
derive a policy that

00:27:59.690 --> 00:28:01.230
optimizes this objective.

00:28:01.230 --> 00:28:03.990
But this is something that
we have many tools to do.

00:28:03.990 --> 00:28:09.080
Maybe to give you
some understanding

00:28:09.080 --> 00:28:12.140
to when inverse reinforcement
learning might be useful.

00:28:12.140 --> 00:28:15.040
So take the example of
maybe learning how to drive,

00:28:15.040 --> 00:28:16.060
autonomous driving.

00:28:16.060 --> 00:28:21.880
It is, I would say, hard
to specify numerically

00:28:21.880 --> 00:28:24.220
what good driving means.

00:28:24.220 --> 00:28:27.080
First of all, it's hard in
general, but also because each

00:28:27.080 --> 00:28:29.400
and every one of us has a
different interpretation

00:28:29.400 --> 00:28:31.280
of what good driving means.

00:28:31.280 --> 00:28:34.200
And so in this way, what
we would be doing is

00:28:34.200 --> 00:28:39.320
we would be collecting
examples of people driving.

00:28:39.320 --> 00:28:41.720
And this is actually easy
to collect because you just

00:28:41.720 --> 00:28:42.960
let people drive.

00:28:42.960 --> 00:28:45.000
And then from that,
you would be deriving

00:28:45.000 --> 00:28:47.600
a sort of analytical
expression that tells us

00:28:47.600 --> 00:28:51.840
what these people thought it
was relating with good driving

00:28:51.840 --> 00:28:53.220
and then optimize for that.

00:29:01.080 --> 00:29:04.220
And so looking at
the first of the two,

00:29:04.220 --> 00:29:08.150
so behavior cloning, I would
say the main idea of behavior

00:29:08.150 --> 00:29:11.610
cloning is, in some way,
particularly simple.

00:29:11.610 --> 00:29:14.470
So the main ideas that we
want to train a policy through

00:29:14.470 --> 00:29:15.670
supervised learning.

00:29:15.670 --> 00:29:19.430
So the skeleton of an imitation
learning algorithm typically

00:29:19.430 --> 00:29:22.490
goes that we have a data set
of expert demonstrations.

00:29:22.490 --> 00:29:26.270
Again, we have someone
teleoperate our robot.

00:29:26.270 --> 00:29:28.550
And then we basically
just train our policy

00:29:28.550 --> 00:29:33.670
to mimic this expert through
any of our favorite supervised

00:29:33.670 --> 00:29:34.870
learning methods.

00:29:34.870 --> 00:29:37.710
So maybe minimizing or doing
maximum likelihood estimation

00:29:37.710 --> 00:29:41.230
on that data set.

00:29:41.230 --> 00:29:46.990
And I would say one of the
very first examples of working

00:29:46.990 --> 00:29:52.190
behavior cloning in practice
dates back to the late 1980s.

00:29:52.190 --> 00:29:55.950
If I remember correctly,
this is a study from CMU.

00:29:55.950 --> 00:29:59.590
And the goal here was
to basically learn

00:29:59.590 --> 00:30:05.680
how to drive from examples
of human striving.

00:30:05.680 --> 00:30:07.660
So there was this
military vehicle.

00:30:07.660 --> 00:30:09.460
And the problem
that they set up was

00:30:09.460 --> 00:30:12.020
in some way kind of
resembling to what

00:30:12.020 --> 00:30:16.240
I would say many of also
recent approaches are doing.

00:30:16.240 --> 00:30:18.240
So they're basically
recording some images,

00:30:18.240 --> 00:30:20.340
maybe a front camera
images on the vehicle

00:30:20.340 --> 00:30:23.580
or other cameras
around the vehicles,

00:30:23.580 --> 00:30:28.460
and they're basically mapping
those cameras to the steering

00:30:28.460 --> 00:30:31.042
angle for the vehicle.

00:30:31.042 --> 00:30:32.500
And specifically,
at the time, they

00:30:32.500 --> 00:30:38.060
were doing this by learning a
small neural network to do this.

00:30:38.060 --> 00:30:42.740
And for the times, this was
actually a pretty amazing result

00:30:42.740 --> 00:30:47.260
because they got the vehicle
to drive on fairly easy roads

00:30:47.260 --> 00:30:48.960
and controlled environments.

00:30:48.960 --> 00:30:51.980
But still, kind
of this, I think,

00:30:51.980 --> 00:30:55.180
really maps out
also the skeleton

00:30:55.180 --> 00:30:58.260
of what many of the most
current methods are doing,

00:30:58.260 --> 00:31:00.900
that is trying out to
set the behavior cloning

00:31:00.900 --> 00:31:04.540
problem as something that goes
from a high dimensional input,

00:31:04.540 --> 00:31:10.160
for example, image space, to
control that we care about.

00:31:10.160 --> 00:31:14.300
And so you might ask, like this
was happening in the 1980s,

00:31:14.300 --> 00:31:15.340
1990s.

00:31:15.340 --> 00:31:18.800
So is this all that there is
to it, to imitation learning,

00:31:18.800 --> 00:31:20.440
or is there more?

00:31:20.440 --> 00:31:22.840
And in particular,
is this simple recipe

00:31:22.840 --> 00:31:24.600
guaranteed to work?

00:31:24.600 --> 00:31:26.300
And the spoiler
is, of course, no.

00:31:26.300 --> 00:31:30.880
There are a number of details
that come with these techniques.

00:31:30.880 --> 00:31:32.960
And I would say the
main factor here

00:31:32.960 --> 00:31:35.680
is that imitation
learning is fundamentally

00:31:35.680 --> 00:31:37.720
different from plain
supervised learning

00:31:37.720 --> 00:31:40.640
for a number of different ways.

00:31:40.640 --> 00:31:44.540
Specifically, we saw before how
supervised learning at its core

00:31:44.540 --> 00:31:48.200
is basically a mechanism to
learn a mapping from input data

00:31:48.200 --> 00:31:53.960
to output labels based on a data
set of examples of this mapping.

00:31:53.960 --> 00:31:56.240
On the other hand,
imitation learning,

00:31:56.240 --> 00:32:00.790
while it does build heavily on
specifically behavior cloning

00:32:00.790 --> 00:32:03.310
builds heavily on
supervised learning,

00:32:03.310 --> 00:32:05.510
it has some key differences.

00:32:05.510 --> 00:32:10.070
And specifically one is that
in the case of deploying

00:32:10.070 --> 00:32:12.230
an autonomous system
in the real world,

00:32:12.230 --> 00:32:15.190
even small prediction errors
will compound over time.

00:32:15.190 --> 00:32:18.190
And so you can
imagine a situation

00:32:18.190 --> 00:32:26.350
where we have maybe a data
set that was showing us

00:32:26.350 --> 00:32:28.370
a certain behavior
of our system.

00:32:28.370 --> 00:32:38.110
So going from x0, taking
u0, x1, u1, and so forth.

00:32:38.110 --> 00:32:42.430
At deployment time, if we were
to start from the same x0,

00:32:42.430 --> 00:32:45.590
regardless of how
good our model is,

00:32:45.590 --> 00:32:48.230
there are always going to be
most likely some prediction

00:32:48.230 --> 00:32:48.730
error.

00:32:48.730 --> 00:32:54.230
And so maybe this will lead
us into defining a u0 hat that

00:32:54.230 --> 00:32:57.190
is slightly different from the
original u0, which leads us

00:32:57.190 --> 00:32:59.940
to a slightly different x1.

00:32:59.940 --> 00:33:04.700
And now, we are in a state
that is potentially slightly

00:33:04.700 --> 00:33:07.680
outside of the support
of our data distribution,

00:33:07.680 --> 00:33:10.300
which will lead to having
a following prediction that

00:33:10.300 --> 00:33:12.420
is even slightly more off.

00:33:12.420 --> 00:33:16.220
And so you can see how this
compounding effect is basically

00:33:16.220 --> 00:33:18.320
getting exponentially
worse over time.

00:33:18.320 --> 00:33:23.140
Because we are-- since in the
context of autonomous systems

00:33:23.140 --> 00:33:26.780
and imitation learning,
we are active agents

00:33:26.780 --> 00:33:29.740
in the environment,
our errors will

00:33:29.740 --> 00:33:34.320
lead to denote the distribution
of states that we observe.

00:33:36.805 --> 00:33:38.180
And this is
something that is not

00:33:38.180 --> 00:33:40.360
present in plain
supervised learning.

00:33:45.940 --> 00:33:48.320
Another reason for--
does this make sense?

00:33:51.660 --> 00:33:54.700
So another reason for why
imitation learning is also

00:33:54.700 --> 00:33:57.050
a difference is that in
general, since here, we

00:33:57.050 --> 00:33:59.470
might be dealing with
physical systems,

00:33:59.470 --> 00:34:03.770
there might be some physical
constraints or dependencies that

00:34:03.770 --> 00:34:05.570
are basically unique
to the fact that we're

00:34:05.570 --> 00:34:08.110
dealing with some real
world embodied system.

00:34:08.110 --> 00:34:11.650
So if we're just learning a
mapping from camera images

00:34:11.650 --> 00:34:15.409
to object classes,
then we literally

00:34:15.409 --> 00:34:18.489
have no limits to what
those object classes

00:34:18.489 --> 00:34:20.992
can be within that vocabulary.

00:34:20.992 --> 00:34:22.409
On the other hand,
if we're trying

00:34:22.409 --> 00:34:26.630
to learn to predict the joint
angles of a robot manipulator,

00:34:26.630 --> 00:34:28.770
we have some constraints
that we have to adhere to.

00:34:28.770 --> 00:34:31.429
So we cannot output
any joint angle.

00:34:34.929 --> 00:34:38.469
Along this line, in
general, the domain,

00:34:38.469 --> 00:34:40.810
the source domain may be
different from the one

00:34:40.810 --> 00:34:41.870
we have at deployment.

00:34:41.870 --> 00:34:44.090
So say that we are
trying to learn

00:34:44.090 --> 00:34:47.610
how to control a humanoid by
looking at videos of humans

00:34:47.610 --> 00:34:50.330
doing different actions.

00:34:50.330 --> 00:34:53.730
There is, of course,
an embodiment gap.

00:34:53.730 --> 00:34:56.550
And I would say most
of these problems boil

00:34:56.550 --> 00:34:59.370
down to what is known
as covariate shift,

00:34:59.370 --> 00:35:02.170
and we'll talk more
about this on Monday.

00:35:02.170 --> 00:35:05.750
But the idea is that the
state distribution that

00:35:05.750 --> 00:35:09.990
is induced, that is
observed through the expert,

00:35:09.990 --> 00:35:12.470
by state distribution,
I mean the set of states

00:35:12.470 --> 00:35:17.110
that we are observing while kind
of seeing the expert behave,

00:35:17.110 --> 00:35:18.910
will be different
from the set of states

00:35:18.910 --> 00:35:23.990
that we're observing as we
ourselves are taking decisions.

00:35:23.990 --> 00:35:26.510
And so this leads to
a distribution shift

00:35:26.510 --> 00:35:30.230
that is uniquely not there in
the plain supervised learning

00:35:30.230 --> 00:35:31.750
setting.

00:35:31.750 --> 00:35:35.990
And last but not least, if
collecting more data of images

00:35:35.990 --> 00:35:38.490
might be extremely cheap,
we just scrape the internet.

00:35:38.490 --> 00:35:41.470
Collecting demonstrations
or useful demonstrations

00:35:41.470 --> 00:35:44.470
in the case of robotics is
usually quite more expensive

00:35:44.470 --> 00:35:46.930
because you have to maybe
drive your car around.

00:35:52.080 --> 00:35:52.580
OK.

00:35:52.580 --> 00:35:54.660
And so on Monday,
we'll basically

00:35:54.660 --> 00:35:57.580
start looking at these
challenges and mechanisms

00:35:57.580 --> 00:36:01.220
to approach them.

00:36:01.220 --> 00:36:02.720
Any questions so far?

00:36:10.785 --> 00:36:13.980
So let's also say a couple of
words on inverse reinforcement

00:36:13.980 --> 00:36:14.660
learning.

00:36:14.660 --> 00:36:18.810
So as we were saying before, in
inverse reinforcement learning,

00:36:18.810 --> 00:36:21.060
we should basically see this
as an orthogonal approach

00:36:21.060 --> 00:36:23.420
to imitation
learning, where we are

00:36:23.420 --> 00:36:27.860
trying to recover our reward
function from demonstrations

00:36:27.860 --> 00:36:29.540
of our policy.

00:36:29.540 --> 00:36:35.800
And as an example, just
to ground the intuition,

00:36:35.800 --> 00:36:37.820
we could, for example,
consider the fact

00:36:37.820 --> 00:36:39.740
where we have a
warehouse robot that

00:36:39.740 --> 00:36:42.000
must navigate from a starting
point to a goal point,

00:36:42.000 --> 00:36:43.897
similarly to what
we're saying here.

00:36:43.897 --> 00:36:45.480
And in the case of
behavioral cloning,

00:36:45.480 --> 00:36:47.580
we would be collecting
demonstrations

00:36:47.580 --> 00:36:53.330
of examples of how to go
from A to B and then train

00:36:53.330 --> 00:36:55.350
a policy that can
imitate that behavior.

00:36:55.350 --> 00:36:57.570
Now, this policy will
probably be quite effective

00:36:57.570 --> 00:36:59.850
in similar scenarios,
for example,

00:36:59.850 --> 00:37:03.890
going from the same state
and goal combination.

00:37:03.890 --> 00:37:06.730
But in the moment where we
might be maybe reassembling

00:37:06.730 --> 00:37:10.150
the configuration
of the warehouse,

00:37:10.150 --> 00:37:13.170
then this might find
some limitations.

00:37:13.170 --> 00:37:17.250
On the other hand, if we
understand the overall goal

00:37:17.250 --> 00:37:21.310
of the expert from which
we have demonstrations,

00:37:21.310 --> 00:37:24.550
then we have basically
a representation,

00:37:24.550 --> 00:37:26.170
in this case, the
reward function,

00:37:26.170 --> 00:37:31.050
that can be potentially
much more generalizable.

00:37:31.050 --> 00:37:32.850
For example, something
along the lines

00:37:32.850 --> 00:37:35.490
of moving closer
to the goal is good

00:37:35.490 --> 00:37:38.650
and moving closer to
the obstacles is bad.

00:37:38.650 --> 00:37:41.490
Something that can be seen
as a sort of vector field

00:37:41.490 --> 00:37:43.670
in the space of possible paths.

00:37:46.040 --> 00:37:48.290
Now, this is not to say that
the inverse reinforcement

00:37:48.290 --> 00:37:50.880
learning is generally
better, but just

00:37:50.880 --> 00:37:54.840
to highlight a potential kind of
dissimilarity between these two

00:37:54.840 --> 00:37:57.040
methods.

00:37:57.040 --> 00:37:59.640
And we will see again
kind of a couple

00:37:59.640 --> 00:38:03.420
of specific instantiations
of reinforcement learning.

00:38:10.680 --> 00:38:16.560
So let's now introduce some
key terminology and notation

00:38:16.560 --> 00:38:18.420
for reinforcement learning.

00:38:20.960 --> 00:38:24.160
And at a high level
kind of fundamentally,

00:38:24.160 --> 00:38:26.160
I would say reinforcement
learning can

00:38:26.160 --> 00:38:29.440
be seen as a formalism for
learning-based decision

00:38:29.440 --> 00:38:33.240
making, where it is basically
an approach for learning

00:38:33.240 --> 00:38:36.120
how to make decisions
from experience.

00:38:36.120 --> 00:38:41.520
And this happens by basically
following a basic learning loop

00:38:41.520 --> 00:38:43.600
and interaction
between two agents.

00:38:43.600 --> 00:38:47.350
The first one is what our
policy or here referred to

00:38:47.350 --> 00:38:48.790
as the agent.

00:38:48.790 --> 00:38:50.830
And then we have an
environment, which

00:38:50.830 --> 00:38:53.830
is basically the
world or our system

00:38:53.830 --> 00:38:56.110
that we're interacting with.

00:38:56.110 --> 00:38:58.310
And the interaction
between these two elements

00:38:58.310 --> 00:39:03.590
usually goes such that we have
the agent observing some state.

00:39:03.590 --> 00:39:05.710
The agent will take
this state in order

00:39:05.710 --> 00:39:09.270
to compute an action or control.

00:39:09.270 --> 00:39:12.390
This action will
be kind of acted

00:39:12.390 --> 00:39:15.250
in the environment,
which on the other hand,

00:39:15.250 --> 00:39:17.510
will return to us
with an updated

00:39:17.510 --> 00:39:21.630
state of the system and a
scalar measure of performance

00:39:21.630 --> 00:39:24.070
or reward, rt.

00:39:24.070 --> 00:39:27.470
And if you see this loop
unrolling over time,

00:39:27.470 --> 00:39:31.030
this leads to the definition
of a trajectory tau, which

00:39:31.030 --> 00:39:34.790
is nothing else than the kind
of unrolled sequence of states

00:39:34.790 --> 00:39:38.490
and controls and rewards that
we are observing over time.

00:39:42.070 --> 00:39:44.190
And the point in
reinforcement learning

00:39:44.190 --> 00:39:47.530
is that basically, all
reinforcement learning

00:39:47.530 --> 00:39:52.050
algorithms out there will
basically use these trajectories

00:39:52.050 --> 00:39:55.410
in order to improve the policy.

00:39:55.410 --> 00:39:59.390
And so when we were referring to
as learning by trial and error,

00:39:59.390 --> 00:40:00.350
this is what we meant.

00:40:00.350 --> 00:40:02.650
Because reinforcement
learning essentially

00:40:02.650 --> 00:40:06.890
defines this
interaction, where we

00:40:06.890 --> 00:40:10.130
have an agent that is an
active agent in our system,

00:40:10.130 --> 00:40:12.490
tries out different
strategies, and we'll

00:40:12.490 --> 00:40:16.330
use the trajectories that
result from its exploration

00:40:16.330 --> 00:40:20.230
to maximize some notion
of score and performance.

00:40:25.810 --> 00:40:29.330
And so in reinforcement learning
literature, which generally

00:40:29.330 --> 00:40:32.950
originates from the
computer science literature,

00:40:32.950 --> 00:40:35.170
there are a number of
terminologies that I

00:40:35.170 --> 00:40:38.050
think is good to set in place.

00:40:38.050 --> 00:40:41.090
So we'll refer to as policy
as a mapping from state

00:40:41.090 --> 00:40:44.800
to actions, that basically
defines the agent's behavior.

00:40:44.800 --> 00:40:48.980
And this typically is expressed,
at least in the RL literature,

00:40:48.980 --> 00:40:52.800
as a distribution over
controls given states.

00:40:52.800 --> 00:40:55.720
So so far, we were mostly
assuming that the policy

00:40:55.720 --> 00:40:57.640
was a deterministic policy.

00:40:57.640 --> 00:41:04.140
So something that basically
led to a control given a state.

00:41:04.140 --> 00:41:06.560
But in the RL literature,
this is usually

00:41:06.560 --> 00:41:13.500
seen as a distribution
over controls given states.

00:41:16.400 --> 00:41:22.600
So in practice, if we
have our gridworld example

00:41:22.600 --> 00:41:25.480
for navigation, our
possible actions

00:41:25.480 --> 00:41:28.460
might be the four
cardinal directions.

00:41:31.480 --> 00:41:33.440
And in this case,
the policy will then

00:41:33.440 --> 00:41:39.840
be representing a
distribution over these four

00:41:39.840 --> 00:41:42.790
cardinal directions in the
case of a discrete action

00:41:42.790 --> 00:41:45.270
space in this case.

00:41:45.270 --> 00:41:50.870
Similarly, if we were
representing a continuous action

00:41:50.870 --> 00:41:57.350
space, we could potentially
use a Gaussian distribution

00:41:57.350 --> 00:42:00.250
over candidate actions here.

00:42:09.630 --> 00:42:12.230
When we refer to
as environment, we

00:42:12.230 --> 00:42:15.970
will basically refer to as the
system the agent interacts with.

00:42:15.970 --> 00:42:18.630
Mathematically, this is
represented as, again,

00:42:18.630 --> 00:42:21.130
conditional distribution
over the next states

00:42:21.130 --> 00:42:24.910
given the current states
and the current actions.

00:42:24.910 --> 00:42:28.970
The reward is a scalar
measure of immediate success.

00:42:28.970 --> 00:42:32.710
So something that given
a state and a control,

00:42:32.710 --> 00:42:35.830
will give us a scalar measure
of how good that control was

00:42:35.830 --> 00:42:37.090
in that specific state.

00:42:40.860 --> 00:42:44.540
And we will be very often
talking about value functions.

00:42:44.540 --> 00:42:50.460
So if on one hand the reward
measures immediate success.

00:42:50.460 --> 00:42:55.100
So again, how good was this
action in a specific state?

00:42:55.100 --> 00:42:57.260
A value function
represents a measure

00:42:57.260 --> 00:43:00.060
of performance in the long run.

00:43:00.060 --> 00:43:02.100
That is specifically,
it will define

00:43:02.100 --> 00:43:06.700
how much the agent can expect
to achieve in the future.

00:43:06.700 --> 00:43:10.940
So I would say
again, visually, say

00:43:10.940 --> 00:43:16.640
that we have our usual kind
of pictorial trajectory here,

00:43:16.640 --> 00:43:20.220
kind of the reward function
is something that is defined

00:43:20.220 --> 00:43:22.940
over the immediate transition.

00:43:22.940 --> 00:43:27.980
So given a state and a control.

00:43:27.980 --> 00:43:32.380
But at this state,
depending on the uncertainty

00:43:32.380 --> 00:43:34.720
that might come from the policy.

00:43:34.720 --> 00:43:37.720
So here, we might sample
different actions transactions,

00:43:37.720 --> 00:43:40.500
or the state transition
might also be stochastic.

00:43:40.500 --> 00:43:42.240
So given the same
action, we might also

00:43:42.240 --> 00:43:43.880
end up in different states.

00:43:43.880 --> 00:43:46.920
So there is, if you
wish, like a whole set

00:43:46.920 --> 00:43:51.360
of possible evolutions of
the state that we could have.

00:43:51.360 --> 00:43:53.800
So the value
function is something

00:43:53.800 --> 00:43:57.960
that is interested in the
expected value of the reward

00:43:57.960 --> 00:44:01.100
that we can expect to
have given a policy.

00:44:05.360 --> 00:44:09.600
And the reason why I
would say basically most

00:44:09.600 --> 00:44:13.720
or almost all the algorithms in
reinforcement learning really

00:44:13.720 --> 00:44:17.840
focus a lot on the estimating
value functions is because this

00:44:17.840 --> 00:44:21.920
is really the golden
standard that we

00:44:21.920 --> 00:44:23.880
wish to work with
when taking decisions.

00:44:23.880 --> 00:44:26.580
We don't really care about
immediate success, most likely,

00:44:26.580 --> 00:44:29.860
but we care about the
overall success in our task.

00:44:36.030 --> 00:44:37.050
Does this make sense?

00:44:42.510 --> 00:44:45.430
And optionally, we'll
be talking about models.

00:44:45.430 --> 00:44:49.070
Namely, the models for
the agent will essentially

00:44:49.070 --> 00:44:51.270
be representing
its understanding

00:44:51.270 --> 00:44:52.370
of the environment.

00:44:52.370 --> 00:44:56.070
Or for us, it will be
basically an approximate model

00:44:56.070 --> 00:44:58.250
of the system dynamics.

00:44:58.250 --> 00:45:00.990
Mathematically, this
will be represented

00:45:00.990 --> 00:45:03.950
as also a distribution
over next states

00:45:03.950 --> 00:45:06.170
given current states
and current actions,

00:45:06.170 --> 00:45:09.070
but this will basically
represent our understanding

00:45:09.070 --> 00:45:10.910
of the environment.

00:45:10.910 --> 00:45:14.270
This model might
be learned, namely

00:45:14.270 --> 00:45:17.610
we might choose or not to
approximate this model.

00:45:17.610 --> 00:45:20.590
Meaning we might want to
design a strategy that

00:45:20.590 --> 00:45:23.630
leverages this approximate
understanding of our environment

00:45:23.630 --> 00:45:25.390
or simply choose to ignore it.

00:45:25.390 --> 00:45:27.648
And so in the case of
reinforcement learning,

00:45:27.648 --> 00:45:29.190
this is basically
what differentiates

00:45:29.190 --> 00:45:31.990
between what are called as
model-free reinforcement

00:45:31.990 --> 00:45:34.780
learning algorithms and
model-based reinforcement

00:45:34.780 --> 00:45:35.800
learning algorithms.

00:45:42.580 --> 00:45:46.460
But then the question
is why should we

00:45:46.460 --> 00:45:48.460
go for reinforcement
learning, and what

00:45:48.460 --> 00:45:50.540
are the kind of
redeeming characteristics

00:45:50.540 --> 00:45:52.220
of these techniques?

00:45:52.220 --> 00:45:55.723
And I would say one
clear kind of winner

00:45:55.723 --> 00:45:57.140
in terms of
reinforcement learning

00:45:57.140 --> 00:46:00.380
for me is the fact that
reinforcement learning can

00:46:00.380 --> 00:46:03.240
discover new and
unexpected solutions.

00:46:03.240 --> 00:46:06.180
So along the lines of what your
colleague was also alluding to,

00:46:06.180 --> 00:46:07.760
in the case of
imitation learning,

00:46:07.760 --> 00:46:10.580
we're basically at
least in principle

00:46:10.580 --> 00:46:13.960
bounded by the quality of the
demonstrations that we have.

00:46:13.960 --> 00:46:19.420
So we're basically trying
to distill some knowledge

00:46:19.420 --> 00:46:21.140
or behavior that we
think is good enough

00:46:21.140 --> 00:46:24.740
or optimal in the form
of a learned mapping

00:46:24.740 --> 00:46:26.460
by trying to
approximate this mapping

00:46:26.460 --> 00:46:28.720
explicitly in
reinforcement learning.

00:46:28.720 --> 00:46:32.410
We want to discover what are
good solutions by maximizing

00:46:32.410 --> 00:46:34.230
some notion of performance.

00:46:34.230 --> 00:46:37.610
And so this is potentially
unbounded by any teacher

00:46:37.610 --> 00:46:39.370
simply because
there is no teacher.

00:46:39.370 --> 00:46:44.290
And so I think a good example
here is some of the most recent

00:46:44.290 --> 00:46:48.170
successes in reinforcement
learning started in the mid

00:46:48.170 --> 00:46:52.850
2010s with game playing.

00:46:52.850 --> 00:46:56.490
And here, the agent
was essentially

00:46:56.490 --> 00:47:01.650
trying to solve this pong
game in the Atari family.

00:47:01.650 --> 00:47:05.190
And it started basically trying
around different strategies.

00:47:05.190 --> 00:47:07.550
At the beginning it
was not that good.

00:47:07.550 --> 00:47:10.490
But then by where the
goal here was essentially

00:47:10.490 --> 00:47:17.690
to learn how to control
this while trying

00:47:17.690 --> 00:47:21.130
to maximize the score
as a notion of reward.

00:47:21.130 --> 00:47:25.430
But then over time by basically
trying out different strategies,

00:47:25.430 --> 00:47:30.790
it was starting to become
more and s professional.

00:47:30.790 --> 00:47:35.110
Until I think after some time,
kind of the interesting aspect

00:47:35.110 --> 00:47:37.510
that turned out is that
the model kind of found

00:47:37.510 --> 00:47:41.790
an interesting strategy, which
is basically one of digging

00:47:41.790 --> 00:47:45.630
a tunnel here on the
side of the screen,

00:47:45.630 --> 00:47:48.030
because it turned out that
this is an optimal way to

00:47:48.030 --> 00:47:50.850
or a very smart way to
easily get a lot of points.

00:47:50.850 --> 00:47:55.030
And so this is obviously
in the case of games,

00:47:55.030 --> 00:47:56.590
but you might have
heard about one

00:47:56.590 --> 00:47:59.110
of the famous successes
of reinforcement learning

00:47:59.110 --> 00:48:02.230
is the case of AlphaGo,
where basically, there

00:48:02.230 --> 00:48:06.470
was a model trained to
play the game of Go.

00:48:06.470 --> 00:48:11.350
And it ultimately defeated
the world champion

00:48:11.350 --> 00:48:15.190
Lee Sedol in a famous match.

00:48:15.190 --> 00:48:17.410
And there was this move 37.

00:48:17.410 --> 00:48:18.850
I really advise you to check.

00:48:18.850 --> 00:48:23.670
There's a full documentary on
this about this project, where

00:48:23.670 --> 00:48:25.990
move 37 was one that
really stunned even

00:48:25.990 --> 00:48:28.780
most of the experts that
couldn't really would have never

00:48:28.780 --> 00:48:32.380
thought about this move.

00:48:32.380 --> 00:48:34.100
And so this is
really what I mean

00:48:34.100 --> 00:48:36.440
by discovering new solutions.

00:48:36.440 --> 00:48:38.700
Because here, we're only
we're not really bounded

00:48:38.700 --> 00:48:40.805
by demonstrations, but
we are, in the case

00:48:40.805 --> 00:48:42.180
of reinforcement
learning, trying

00:48:42.180 --> 00:48:46.500
to maximize some
notion of score.

00:48:46.500 --> 00:48:49.940
Another reason for why I think
reinforcement learning is

00:48:49.940 --> 00:48:54.020
particularly powerful is
that reinforcement learning

00:48:54.020 --> 00:48:58.620
algorithms are very effective
at optimizing complex objectives

00:48:58.620 --> 00:49:02.820
or kind of complex reward
functions definitions.

00:49:02.820 --> 00:49:06.120
And this makes it appealing
for complex physical tasks.

00:49:06.120 --> 00:49:10.180
So in this case,
doing locomotion

00:49:10.180 --> 00:49:14.180
of a quadruped on rough terrain
is an extremely hard problem

00:49:14.180 --> 00:49:16.860
to solve through
maybe analytical tools

00:49:16.860 --> 00:49:20.180
or of optimization-based
control tools.

00:49:20.180 --> 00:49:22.680
But on the other hand,
reinforcement learning is now,

00:49:22.680 --> 00:49:26.810
I would say, the de facto
standard for basically a robot

00:49:26.810 --> 00:49:28.050
locomotion.

00:49:28.050 --> 00:49:29.770
Because you can
basically leverage

00:49:29.770 --> 00:49:32.590
these highly parallelizable
simulation environments,

00:49:32.590 --> 00:49:37.450
let the robots learn how to
navigate in these environments

00:49:37.450 --> 00:49:40.890
without having to rely on
assumptions on contact dynamics

00:49:40.890 --> 00:49:43.890
and trying to estimate
the models of your system

00:49:43.890 --> 00:49:45.050
and so forth.

00:49:45.050 --> 00:49:47.068
And so this is
really a field where

00:49:47.068 --> 00:49:48.610
reinforcement learning
is turning out

00:49:48.610 --> 00:49:50.670
to be extremely powerful.

00:49:54.290 --> 00:49:55.620
Yes.

00:49:55.620 --> 00:49:58.290
[INAUDIBLE] the kind of model,
the contact dynamics anyway

00:49:58.290 --> 00:50:01.170
for the model that
gets [INAUDIBLE]

00:50:01.170 --> 00:50:05.310
So the simulator internally
is a physics-based simulator,

00:50:05.310 --> 00:50:10.890
so they do have some at the
very least very accurate model

00:50:10.890 --> 00:50:12.690
of the physics.

00:50:12.690 --> 00:50:14.750
But these might be
very expensive right.

00:50:14.750 --> 00:50:18.370
So here, you're basically
trying to distill this physics

00:50:18.370 --> 00:50:20.530
knowledge into a policy.

00:50:20.530 --> 00:50:23.170
So the policy itself is just
a neural net that basically

00:50:23.170 --> 00:50:26.333
maps inputs to
outputs most likely,

00:50:26.333 --> 00:50:28.500
and it's not really taking
into account the physics,

00:50:28.500 --> 00:50:32.100
but it's learned them implicitly
through the simulator,

00:50:32.100 --> 00:50:32.900
if it makes sense.

00:50:35.830 --> 00:50:38.960
[INAUDIBLE]

00:50:38.960 --> 00:50:40.300
Yeah, exactly.

00:50:46.120 --> 00:50:50.000
And so when we look at
reinforcement learning,

00:50:50.000 --> 00:50:51.840
I think it's nice
to highlight how

00:50:51.840 --> 00:50:56.280
it is different from other
machine learning paradigms.

00:50:56.280 --> 00:50:58.940
So first of all, we
highlighted this already.

00:50:58.940 --> 00:51:02.040
So in reinforcement learning, we
don't have a supervision signal,

00:51:02.040 --> 00:51:02.540
right?

00:51:02.540 --> 00:51:04.340
We only have a reward function.

00:51:04.340 --> 00:51:08.800
There is nobody that teaches us
what is a good action to take.

00:51:08.800 --> 00:51:10.320
We only have a
measure that tells

00:51:10.320 --> 00:51:16.760
us how good is an
action we try to take.

00:51:16.760 --> 00:51:21.480
In general, depending on the
task, feedback can be delayed.

00:51:21.480 --> 00:51:24.100
Take the example of
learning how to play chess

00:51:24.100 --> 00:51:26.140
through reinforcement learning.

00:51:26.140 --> 00:51:29.300
It is kind of hard
to give credit

00:51:29.300 --> 00:51:33.900
to one specific move in a chess
game until we reach the end.

00:51:33.900 --> 00:51:38.380
So the only grounded
reward signal that we get

00:51:38.380 --> 00:51:40.340
is whether we win
or not the game.

00:51:40.340 --> 00:51:43.660
But then accrediting this
to any specific moves

00:51:43.660 --> 00:51:45.020
becomes challenging.

00:51:45.020 --> 00:51:47.940
And so this is what I mean
by delayed rewards, which

00:51:47.940 --> 00:51:51.215
creates this problem of
credit assignment, which

00:51:51.215 --> 00:51:53.340
is one of the fundamental
problems in reinforcement

00:51:53.340 --> 00:51:53.840
learning.

00:51:53.840 --> 00:51:56.060
And so we'll see how
different algorithms basically

00:51:56.060 --> 00:51:59.340
try to approach this through the
notion of value functions most

00:51:59.340 --> 00:51:59.880
likely.

00:52:03.100 --> 00:52:06.440
And then we also
hinted at this briefly.

00:52:06.440 --> 00:52:10.080
But in reinforcement
learning data is not IID.

00:52:10.080 --> 00:52:12.460
So the IID assumptions,
I would say

00:52:12.460 --> 00:52:14.660
is one of the most
fundamental assumptions

00:52:14.660 --> 00:52:17.140
in statistical learning theory.

00:52:17.140 --> 00:52:19.860
Basically any theorem
out there assumes

00:52:19.860 --> 00:52:23.870
that the data is independent
and identically distributed,

00:52:23.870 --> 00:52:27.290
and we can basically sample
from the same data generation

00:52:27.290 --> 00:52:28.370
process.

00:52:28.370 --> 00:52:31.110
However, when we work with
reinforcement learning,

00:52:31.110 --> 00:52:34.650
we are indeed in this setting
where the actions we take now

00:52:34.650 --> 00:52:37.250
influence the states we
will see in the future.

00:52:37.250 --> 00:52:39.970
And so this clearly
breaks this assumption

00:52:39.970 --> 00:52:42.450
because the data
is not independent.

00:52:42.450 --> 00:52:47.090
And so this creates for
typically much more unstable

00:52:47.090 --> 00:52:48.970
learning problems.

00:52:48.970 --> 00:52:54.690
And many of the algorithms that
we will see in the next week

00:52:54.690 --> 00:52:57.690
essentially will have
some mechanism in order

00:52:57.690 --> 00:53:01.450
to regularize the learning
problem, partially also

00:53:01.450 --> 00:53:03.470
because of this misalignment.

00:53:10.930 --> 00:53:14.170
Formally, reinforcement
learning builds

00:53:14.170 --> 00:53:17.710
on top of the formalism of
Markov decision processes,

00:53:17.710 --> 00:53:20.040
which we introduced before.

00:53:20.040 --> 00:53:23.760
And specifically,
I would say here

00:53:23.760 --> 00:53:27.280
is where it's also
important that you all

00:53:27.280 --> 00:53:31.960
build kind of a plasticity in
dealing with different notation.

00:53:31.960 --> 00:53:34.000
Because until now we
have been following

00:53:34.000 --> 00:53:37.160
the notation that comes from
the optimal control literature.

00:53:37.160 --> 00:53:40.320
For example, states are
x and controls are u.

00:53:40.320 --> 00:53:42.240
But in the reinforcement
learning literature,

00:53:42.240 --> 00:53:45.640
this typically builds out from
the computer science community,

00:53:45.640 --> 00:53:48.660
which applies just a different
notation for the same objects.

00:53:48.660 --> 00:53:53.560
So instead of being x is in use,
for example, in reinforcement

00:53:53.560 --> 00:53:56.640
learning, you will most
likely be finding states as s

00:53:56.640 --> 00:53:59.200
and actions as a.

00:53:59.200 --> 00:54:01.680
But in general, in order
to be consistent throughout

00:54:01.680 --> 00:54:03.280
this course, we
will keep dealing

00:54:03.280 --> 00:54:07.640
with the notation that comes
from the controls community.

00:54:07.640 --> 00:54:11.320
And so we will be
defining a state space, x,

00:54:11.320 --> 00:54:13.560
that belongs to calligraphic x.

00:54:13.560 --> 00:54:18.390
State space is just the set
of states that we can be in.

00:54:18.390 --> 00:54:21.210
Again, take this
navigation example.

00:54:21.210 --> 00:54:24.430
This would be all of the
cells where we could be in.

00:54:24.430 --> 00:54:25.730
Same thing for the actions.

00:54:25.730 --> 00:54:28.110
This could be a set, the
set of all possible actions

00:54:28.110 --> 00:54:31.710
that we can take
in our environment.

00:54:31.710 --> 00:54:34.150
We have a transition
function or dynamics.

00:54:34.150 --> 00:54:36.310
Usually in the RL
community, this

00:54:36.310 --> 00:54:38.650
is expressed as a
conditional distribution.

00:54:38.650 --> 00:54:42.110
So the distribution over next
states given current states

00:54:42.110 --> 00:54:44.870
and controls we will
have a reward function

00:54:44.870 --> 00:54:47.670
as we discussed before,
and we will have a discount

00:54:47.670 --> 00:54:52.270
factor, which is used
as a mathematical trick

00:54:52.270 --> 00:54:57.190
if you wish to
highlight how much we

00:54:57.190 --> 00:55:03.470
care about immediate reward
against long term reward.

00:55:03.470 --> 00:55:07.270
So I think this is more
clearly explained here

00:55:07.270 --> 00:55:13.550
in this objective, where
specifically, given a Markov

00:55:13.550 --> 00:55:15.690
decision process that
is typically expressed

00:55:15.690 --> 00:55:21.970
as a tuple of elements, the
goal of reinforcement learning

00:55:21.970 --> 00:55:27.650
will be to maximize the RL
objective, which is the expected

00:55:27.650 --> 00:55:30.450
reward, future reward,
or specifically

00:55:30.450 --> 00:55:33.810
the expected discounted
future reward

00:55:33.810 --> 00:55:37.050
under the trajectory
distribution.

00:55:37.050 --> 00:55:39.310
Now, what is this
trajectory distribution?

00:55:39.310 --> 00:55:41.490
Loosely speaking,
this is nothing

00:55:41.490 --> 00:55:44.810
else than the distribution
over all possible trajectories

00:55:44.810 --> 00:55:48.090
that we could ever observe.

00:55:48.090 --> 00:55:50.710
Again, kind of along the lines
of what we were saying here.

00:55:50.710 --> 00:55:54.810
We have some environment with
which we're interacting with.

00:55:54.810 --> 00:55:58.210
The trajectory distribution
is a distribution

00:55:58.210 --> 00:56:01.450
that assigns a probability
to each and every one

00:56:01.450 --> 00:56:03.850
of these possible trajectories.

00:56:03.850 --> 00:56:09.130
Mathematically, this is derived
through these conditional

00:56:09.130 --> 00:56:10.190
independencies.

00:56:10.190 --> 00:56:13.560
So the probability
of trajectory tau

00:56:13.560 --> 00:56:16.920
is basically the probability of
starting from an initial state

00:56:16.920 --> 00:56:21.020
x0 multiplied by the product of.

00:56:21.020 --> 00:56:24.340
Basically the two sources
of randomness that we have,

00:56:24.340 --> 00:56:29.280
where one is just the
randomness over the next state,

00:56:29.280 --> 00:56:31.820
and one is the randomness
given by our policy.

00:56:35.520 --> 00:56:37.580
So given this
trajectory distribution,

00:56:37.580 --> 00:56:41.040
the RL objective will be
to maximize the discounted

00:56:41.040 --> 00:56:46.080
sum of rewards, the
discounted sum of rewards,

00:56:46.080 --> 00:56:48.320
where the expectation is
taken under this trajectory

00:56:48.320 --> 00:56:50.120
distribution.

00:56:50.120 --> 00:56:52.780
The discounting here,
as you might notice,

00:56:52.780 --> 00:56:56.160
is basically a
mechanism for which

00:56:56.160 --> 00:57:02.200
we can give more or less weight
to immediate or future rewards.

00:57:02.200 --> 00:57:04.040
Also, mathematically,
this is something

00:57:04.040 --> 00:57:07.600
that allows us to deal with
infinite horizon problems,

00:57:07.600 --> 00:57:10.520
because if this were to
be an infinite sum, then

00:57:10.520 --> 00:57:13.070
a good luck optimizing this.

00:57:13.070 --> 00:57:17.090
But if we have this
scalar discount factor,

00:57:17.090 --> 00:57:18.890
then this actually
becomes tractable.

00:57:27.870 --> 00:57:31.010
Any questions on
this formulation?

00:57:40.070 --> 00:57:44.510
And so I would say whenever
you will be setting up

00:57:44.510 --> 00:57:48.030
also in your p-sets some
reinforcement learning problems,

00:57:48.030 --> 00:57:50.790
I think the very
first decision to take

00:57:50.790 --> 00:57:55.870
will be to basically define
what an MDP is for our problem.

00:57:55.870 --> 00:57:59.390
And so some examples here,
take the case of chess.

00:57:59.390 --> 00:58:03.950
The state space might be defined
by all the possible positions

00:58:03.950 --> 00:58:05.950
of the pieces on the board.

00:58:05.950 --> 00:58:10.620
The actions will be basically
all the feasible next moves.

00:58:10.620 --> 00:58:12.840
The dynamics here
are deterministic.

00:58:12.840 --> 00:58:16.440
So given a position of
the board and the action,

00:58:16.440 --> 00:58:19.940
then the next state will
just be the next position

00:58:19.940 --> 00:58:22.220
of pieces on the board.

00:58:22.220 --> 00:58:24.860
And the reward function
might be something as simple

00:58:24.860 --> 00:58:27.500
as negative one if we lose
the game or a plus one

00:58:27.500 --> 00:58:28.440
if we win the game.

00:58:31.180 --> 00:58:34.980
Similar thing for quadruped.

00:58:34.980 --> 00:58:37.900
The state might be
the robot's pose.

00:58:37.900 --> 00:58:42.060
And we might be controlling the
joint torque commands for each

00:58:42.060 --> 00:58:44.100
of the legs of the quadrupeds.

00:58:44.100 --> 00:58:46.100
And then the dynamics
here might be

00:58:46.100 --> 00:58:50.420
kind of where we think
the quadruped will

00:58:50.420 --> 00:58:54.500
be given some specific
choice of torques.

00:58:54.500 --> 00:58:57.260
And the reward function, if
we wish for the quadruped

00:58:57.260 --> 00:58:59.520
to basically learn
how to walk fast,

00:58:59.520 --> 00:59:01.540
it will be something
as a positive

00:59:01.540 --> 00:59:04.420
if we're taking a
forward step or something

00:59:04.420 --> 00:59:09.610
negative if we're falling and
then 0 if we're still standing.

00:59:09.610 --> 00:59:11.990
But again, these are
just toy examples.

00:59:11.990 --> 00:59:16.210
But the intuition here is that
whenever we posit our problem

00:59:16.210 --> 00:59:17.750
as a reinforcement
learning problem,

00:59:17.750 --> 00:59:20.210
we will have to necessarily
define the elements that

00:59:20.210 --> 00:59:21.790
define an MDP.

00:59:26.690 --> 00:59:30.050
And so we mentioned
before how value functions

00:59:30.050 --> 00:59:34.010
are really key in defining
reinforcement learning

00:59:34.010 --> 00:59:34.830
algorithms.

00:59:34.830 --> 00:59:38.570
So we will often be dealing
with different versions

00:59:38.570 --> 00:59:43.130
or different instantiations of
the concept of value function.

00:59:43.130 --> 00:59:44.730
Specifically, let
us start from I

00:59:44.730 --> 00:59:46.390
would say the most
intuitive one,

00:59:46.390 --> 00:59:49.010
which is the state value
function also expressed

00:59:49.010 --> 00:59:55.730
as v of x, which
kind of intuitively

00:59:55.730 --> 00:59:59.530
represents the expected
total reward that we expect

00:59:59.530 --> 01:00:02.690
the agent to have if we
start in a specific state

01:00:02.690 --> 01:00:05.990
and act according to
a particular policy.

01:00:05.990 --> 01:00:08.470
So first of all, let's note
that the value functions

01:00:08.470 --> 01:00:11.690
are conditioned on
a specific policy.

01:00:11.690 --> 01:00:16.830
So the future values that we
expect to have depend inherently

01:00:16.830 --> 01:00:19.350
on how we think we will behave.

01:00:19.350 --> 01:00:23.910
So the value function is an
object that depends on a policy.

01:00:23.910 --> 01:00:26.190
In particular, the
state value function

01:00:26.190 --> 01:00:31.830
is a concept that relates
with a specific state.

01:00:31.830 --> 01:00:37.270
So in this case, the
value function of state x1

01:00:37.270 --> 01:00:39.430
will essentially
be the expectations

01:00:39.430 --> 01:00:43.150
of all my discounted
rewards that I expect

01:00:43.150 --> 01:00:46.430
to have given the, well, the
uncertainty of the environment

01:00:46.430 --> 01:00:48.870
itself, but also
the uncertainty that

01:00:48.870 --> 01:00:51.730
comes from the current
policy that I'm following.

01:00:55.270 --> 01:00:58.050
Similarly, the action
state value function,

01:00:58.050 --> 01:01:01.090
also known as Q function,
is on the other hand,

01:01:01.090 --> 01:01:04.420
something that still measures
this expected future total

01:01:04.420 --> 01:01:05.120
reward.

01:01:05.120 --> 01:01:07.980
But this time, it's
not only something

01:01:07.980 --> 01:01:09.820
that depends on
the state, but it

01:01:09.820 --> 01:01:11.540
depends on the
state and the action

01:01:11.540 --> 01:01:13.540
that we take in that state.

01:01:13.540 --> 01:01:16.540
So it will be essentially
the expected total reward

01:01:16.540 --> 01:01:20.100
that we expect the agent to
observe if we start in a state,

01:01:20.100 --> 01:01:22.380
take an action, and
then act according

01:01:22.380 --> 01:01:23.920
to a particular policy.

01:01:29.220 --> 01:01:31.420
Now, you might think
that the distinction here

01:01:31.420 --> 01:01:33.740
is not too drastic.

01:01:33.740 --> 01:01:37.260
It seems that they are basically
approximating the same thing,

01:01:37.260 --> 01:01:41.340
apart from the uncertainty
on the one step action.

01:01:41.340 --> 01:01:44.340
But can you think of a
reason why Q functions

01:01:44.340 --> 01:01:47.620
might be, in a
way, more practical

01:01:47.620 --> 01:01:50.920
for the purpose of control.

01:01:50.920 --> 01:01:51.420
Yes.

01:01:54.640 --> 01:01:58.860
If you want to explore outside
of your current policy?

01:01:58.860 --> 01:02:00.500
What do you mean by that?

01:02:00.500 --> 01:02:07.850
[INAUDIBLE] Why would you like
to deviate from your course

01:02:07.850 --> 01:02:09.190
In that sense, yes, I agree.

01:02:09.190 --> 01:02:11.010
So I think you're
capturing something

01:02:11.010 --> 01:02:15.550
that we'll be discussing
it on next week Wednesday,

01:02:15.550 --> 01:02:18.810
I think, specifically
in value-based methods,

01:02:18.810 --> 01:02:22.195
when we talk about off
policy algorithms, here,

01:02:22.195 --> 01:02:24.570
this will be the mechanism
through which we can basically

01:02:24.570 --> 01:02:27.190
try actions that do not
depend on our policy.

01:02:27.190 --> 01:02:29.090
So in this way, yes.

01:02:29.090 --> 01:02:31.890
But this is not exactly what
I was referring to here.

01:02:31.890 --> 01:02:36.050
Any other ideas?

01:02:36.050 --> 01:02:36.910
Yeah.

01:02:36.910 --> 01:02:39.730
Is it something
along the lines of it

01:02:39.730 --> 01:02:43.313
allows you to predict
for like the next move?

01:02:43.313 --> 01:02:44.730
Like if you're
playing chess, it's

01:02:44.730 --> 01:02:48.410
not just like this is
moving my knight here

01:02:48.410 --> 01:02:51.830
and put me in a position
[INAUDIBLE] like this.

01:02:51.830 --> 01:02:52.330
Yes.

01:02:52.330 --> 01:02:55.970
I think you're capturing exactly
the point here in the sense

01:02:55.970 --> 01:02:59.050
that ultimately,
what we care about

01:02:59.050 --> 01:03:02.480
is having a mechanism to
choose what our next action is.

01:03:02.480 --> 01:03:11.440
If we can estimate the optimal
Q function, meaning the value

01:03:11.440 --> 01:03:15.880
function that represents what
the future estimate of my cost

01:03:15.880 --> 01:03:17.540
is for the optimal function.

01:03:17.540 --> 01:03:25.820
I basically have A function that
I can easily basically optimize

01:03:25.820 --> 01:03:26.320
over.

01:03:26.320 --> 01:03:29.160
So basically, taking an
action would basically

01:03:29.160 --> 01:03:32.220
entail doing an argmax
or my Q function.

01:03:32.220 --> 01:03:36.440
So here is something where
imagine that this is just

01:03:36.440 --> 01:03:37.880
a lookup table.

01:03:37.880 --> 01:03:42.040
I have an object that for
each state and each action

01:03:42.040 --> 01:03:45.480
gives me an estimate of
what the future reward is.

01:03:45.480 --> 01:03:48.240
So basically what I could
do is I could basically

01:03:48.240 --> 01:03:51.520
iterate over all possible
actions in that state

01:03:51.520 --> 01:03:54.880
and then pick the one
with the highest value.

01:03:54.880 --> 01:03:59.340
And so this basic
Q functions are

01:03:59.340 --> 01:04:02.700
critical because they give
us a practical mechanism

01:04:02.700 --> 01:04:05.240
through which to
learn how to behave.

01:04:07.820 --> 01:04:08.880
Does this make sense?

01:04:16.220 --> 01:04:20.620
And so here we are saying
how the value functions

01:04:20.620 --> 01:04:23.620
depend on a specific policy.

01:04:23.620 --> 01:04:27.520
Because of this, value functions
follow a specific ordering.

01:04:27.520 --> 01:04:30.320
So there is a partial ordering
across value functions.

01:04:30.320 --> 01:04:35.060
So depending on how
good the policy is,

01:04:35.060 --> 01:04:39.620
then a value function could
be kind of greater or smaller

01:04:39.620 --> 01:04:41.860
than others.

01:04:41.860 --> 01:04:46.700
And so we can define the concept
of optimal state value functions

01:04:46.700 --> 01:04:51.060
and Q functions as basically
the value functions that

01:04:51.060 --> 01:04:54.220
relate with the
policy that makes

01:04:54.220 --> 01:04:58.890
for the maximum expected reward.

01:04:58.890 --> 01:05:01.210
And these optimal
state value function

01:05:01.210 --> 01:05:04.450
Q functions are basically
the golden standard we would

01:05:04.450 --> 01:05:06.930
be ideally aiming to and
what most of the algorithms

01:05:06.930 --> 01:05:08.150
try to converge to.

01:05:08.150 --> 01:05:12.530
Because once you have this, the
optimal Q function, then picking

01:05:12.530 --> 01:05:23.490
the optimal action is basically
entails doing an argmax over Q.

01:05:23.490 --> 01:05:26.810
And so again we'll dig
into the details next week.

01:05:26.810 --> 01:05:29.370
But in general, I
definitely advise

01:05:29.370 --> 01:05:32.570
you to fixate these
concepts in your mind

01:05:32.570 --> 01:05:37.370
because these are really
at the foundation I

01:05:37.370 --> 01:05:40.190
would say most reinforcement
learning algorithms out there.

01:05:43.170 --> 01:05:46.290
And so we mentioned
reinforcement learning

01:05:46.290 --> 01:05:49.290
algorithms as a plural because
there are many of them.

01:05:49.290 --> 01:05:53.250
And in general, RL algorithms
can be differentiated

01:05:53.250 --> 01:05:54.790
in a number of subclasses.

01:05:54.790 --> 01:05:56.400
So the first
distinction we already

01:05:56.400 --> 01:05:59.280
mentioned, it is
basically divides model 3

01:05:59.280 --> 01:06:01.200
from model based algorithms.

01:06:01.200 --> 01:06:05.800
So are we using
the dynamics or are

01:06:05.800 --> 01:06:08.000
we interested in
using dynamics known

01:06:08.000 --> 01:06:10.080
or approximate for
our algorithms,

01:06:10.080 --> 01:06:12.580
or are we just not
interested in them.

01:06:12.580 --> 01:06:14.920
So we try to learn a
policy without even trying

01:06:14.920 --> 01:06:18.880
to approximate our dynamics.

01:06:18.880 --> 01:06:20.740
Within the model
free algorithms,

01:06:20.740 --> 01:06:24.320
we will see policy optimization
methods, which basically

01:06:24.320 --> 01:06:27.000
are algorithms that try
to directly optimize

01:06:27.000 --> 01:06:29.920
for the RL objective that
we introduced before.

01:06:29.920 --> 01:06:32.160
So we will see how
we are going to try

01:06:32.160 --> 01:06:37.240
to directly cast the RL
problem as a maximization of RL

01:06:37.240 --> 01:06:40.120
objective.

01:06:40.120 --> 01:06:41.640
And then we will
have value based

01:06:41.640 --> 01:06:43.540
methods, which on
the other hand,

01:06:43.540 --> 01:06:47.100
will basically optimize for
this objective implicitly.

01:06:47.100 --> 01:06:50.640
So we won't be facing
taking this at face value,

01:06:50.640 --> 01:06:53.830
but we will be trying to
estimate the optimal value

01:06:53.830 --> 01:06:57.150
functions and then
defining our policy pi

01:06:57.150 --> 01:06:59.770
as an argmax over the
optimal value function.

01:07:05.310 --> 01:07:07.170
On the other side
of the spectrum,

01:07:07.170 --> 01:07:09.310
in model based
algorithms, we will either

01:07:09.310 --> 01:07:10.730
try to estimate the model.

01:07:10.730 --> 01:07:13.350
So we don't know what
the dynamics are,

01:07:13.350 --> 01:07:15.990
but so we try to estimate
it along the lines of system

01:07:15.990 --> 01:07:20.070
identification and then use
these estimate models to improve

01:07:20.070 --> 01:07:21.950
our policies.

01:07:21.950 --> 01:07:24.310
Or we might just know
the model, and then

01:07:24.310 --> 01:07:27.630
this basically boils
down to many of the tools

01:07:27.630 --> 01:07:30.050
we saw in the previous
weeks in this class.

01:07:32.870 --> 01:07:35.390
And so you might
ask yourself kind of

01:07:35.390 --> 01:07:37.830
why so many reinforcement
learning algorithms

01:07:37.830 --> 01:07:41.350
how do we even navigate choosing
between different reinforcement

01:07:41.350 --> 01:07:42.530
learning algorithms.

01:07:42.530 --> 01:07:44.590
And I would say the
reality is that there

01:07:44.590 --> 01:07:46.350
are a number of trade offs.

01:07:46.350 --> 01:07:49.790
So we'll talk a lot about
sample efficiency and stability.

01:07:49.790 --> 01:07:52.770
So by sample efficiency
means how many

01:07:52.770 --> 01:07:54.250
interactions with
the environment

01:07:54.250 --> 01:07:56.970
do I have to do before
I actually converge

01:07:56.970 --> 01:07:58.325
to something that is useful.

01:07:58.325 --> 01:08:00.450
And so we will see how
different algorithms will be

01:08:00.450 --> 01:08:03.810
more or less sample efficient.

01:08:03.810 --> 01:08:07.450
Typically, we will also see
how different algorithms

01:08:07.450 --> 01:08:09.230
are more or less stable.

01:08:09.230 --> 01:08:15.410
So is it easier to learn how
to estimate the optimal value

01:08:15.410 --> 01:08:18.250
function, or is it easier
to directly optimize

01:08:18.250 --> 01:08:20.370
for the RL objective.

01:08:20.370 --> 01:08:23.050
And so different algorithms
will be in different side

01:08:23.050 --> 01:08:24.689
of the spectrum here.

01:08:24.689 --> 01:08:27.529
Of course, also the
assumptions in our environment

01:08:27.529 --> 01:08:28.550
will play a role.

01:08:28.550 --> 01:08:30.750
So are we in an
episodic setting?

01:08:30.750 --> 01:08:33.850
That is can we easily
restart our chess game.

01:08:33.850 --> 01:08:36.090
Or are we in an
infinite horizon setting

01:08:36.090 --> 01:08:37.590
where there are no resets.

01:08:37.590 --> 01:08:41.850
And so depending on this,
what is the right algorithm?

01:08:41.850 --> 01:08:46.050
Are we dealing with a
continuous state or action

01:08:46.050 --> 01:08:47.470
space or a discrete one?

01:08:47.470 --> 01:08:51.800
So different algorithms will
be more or less tailored

01:08:51.800 --> 01:08:53.279
for these cases.

01:08:53.279 --> 01:08:55.100
And then I would say
more fundamentally,

01:08:55.100 --> 01:08:57.640
when we talk about model
3 and model based methods,

01:08:57.640 --> 01:09:00.359
is it easier to represent
the policy directly,

01:09:00.359 --> 01:09:03.080
or is it easier to
represent the model?

01:09:03.080 --> 01:09:05.819
Or again, doing
a simple example,

01:09:05.819 --> 01:09:11.560
take the case where we're trying
to balance the usual cartpole

01:09:11.560 --> 01:09:15.120
example, where we want to keep
this pole upright by controlling

01:09:15.120 --> 01:09:16.840
the cart.

01:09:16.840 --> 01:09:22.920
Now if we wanted to estimate an
approximate model of our system

01:09:22.920 --> 01:09:26.939
dynamics, kind of this,
depending on where the pole is,

01:09:26.939 --> 01:09:31.899
might be an extremely kind of a
non-linear model to approximate.

01:09:31.899 --> 01:09:33.500
And so potentially difficult.

01:09:33.500 --> 01:09:38.600
On the other hand, if we
simply look at how to behave,

01:09:38.600 --> 01:09:41.620
the behavior rule is not
that hard to encode, right.

01:09:41.620 --> 01:09:44.720
So if the pole is going
left, I might also

01:09:44.720 --> 01:09:46.240
want to go left
to counterbalance

01:09:46.240 --> 01:09:47.359
for that movement.

01:09:47.359 --> 01:09:48.990
If the pole is going--

01:09:48.990 --> 01:09:50.965
kind of controlling
the cart based

01:09:50.965 --> 01:09:52.590
on the position of
the pole is actually

01:09:52.590 --> 01:09:56.930
a pretty easy thing to
do or a more mundane.

01:09:56.930 --> 01:09:58.990
Whenever we're all
taking a shower,

01:09:58.990 --> 01:10:00.990
we don't really model
the thermodynamics

01:10:00.990 --> 01:10:05.830
of where the heat propagation is
and how the heat and the water

01:10:05.830 --> 01:10:07.170
is propagating to our body.

01:10:07.170 --> 01:10:09.890
But simply, we feel something
is hot, and we turn the knob.

01:10:09.890 --> 01:10:13.470
So that is actually easier to
control compared to estimating

01:10:13.470 --> 01:10:17.270
the full model of the system.

01:10:17.270 --> 01:10:21.230
And with sample efficiency,
we'll look at off policy

01:10:21.230 --> 01:10:22.970
and on policy algorithms.

01:10:22.970 --> 01:10:25.830
We'll see how there is
a spectrum of algorithms

01:10:25.830 --> 01:10:30.230
from the less efficient
to the more efficient.

01:10:30.230 --> 01:10:31.910
And so the question
there could be

01:10:31.910 --> 01:10:34.770
why even bother using
less efficient algorithms.

01:10:34.770 --> 01:10:37.190
And the point is
that wall clock time

01:10:37.190 --> 01:10:39.650
is not the same as efficiency.

01:10:39.650 --> 01:10:43.790
So typically, kind of less
sample efficient algorithms

01:10:43.790 --> 01:10:46.220
are also the ones
that are more stable.

01:10:46.220 --> 01:10:49.460
And so if we are in a setting
where we can easily parallelize

01:10:49.460 --> 01:10:51.910
or simulate in a
cheap way, then we

01:10:51.910 --> 01:10:53.660
don't really need to
go through the effort

01:10:53.660 --> 01:10:56.700
of having complex and
unstable algorithms because

01:10:56.700 --> 01:10:57.700
of efficiency.

01:10:57.700 --> 01:11:01.220
But actually, we can use
less efficient algorithms,

01:11:01.220 --> 01:11:03.400
but because for us,
experience is very cheap.

01:11:08.220 --> 01:11:09.660
And we will look
at different kind

01:11:09.660 --> 01:11:13.040
of stability and convergence
properties of different methods.

01:11:17.700 --> 01:11:25.060
So to conclude, so one thing
that I want you all to take out

01:11:25.060 --> 01:11:27.040
of this lecture today is that.

01:11:27.040 --> 01:11:29.140
So we have been introducing
and hopefully building

01:11:29.140 --> 01:11:30.740
this mental model
in which we can

01:11:30.740 --> 01:11:33.980
navigate different algorithms
starting next week.

01:11:33.980 --> 01:11:37.460
But I don't want that
the key takeaway here

01:11:37.460 --> 01:11:40.180
is that algorithms belonging
to different buckets

01:11:40.180 --> 01:11:42.380
should be seen as alternatives.

01:11:42.380 --> 01:11:45.840
Specifically, I don't want
that imitation learning

01:11:45.840 --> 01:11:48.310
and reinforcement learning
are seen as alternatives.

01:11:48.310 --> 01:11:50.560
So along the lines of what
your colleague was alluding

01:11:50.560 --> 01:11:54.200
to before, I would
say that nominally,

01:11:54.200 --> 01:11:59.320
a kind of frontier pipelines
and architectures for autonomous

01:11:59.320 --> 01:12:03.100
systems typically rely more and
more on end to end frameworks.

01:12:03.100 --> 01:12:04.980
So these tools are
more and more used.

01:12:04.980 --> 01:12:07.800
So there is a lot of
learning going on.

01:12:07.800 --> 01:12:12.280
But this learning is not done
in either through imitation

01:12:12.280 --> 01:12:14.480
learning or through
reinforcement learning.

01:12:14.480 --> 01:12:19.000
But typically, frontier
pipelines and autonomy stacks

01:12:19.000 --> 01:12:23.100
are essentially built as
scaffolded training pipelines,

01:12:23.100 --> 01:12:25.680
whereby different algorithms
and different techniques

01:12:25.680 --> 01:12:28.920
play roles at different
elements of the stack.

01:12:28.920 --> 01:12:33.960
So for example, this is taking
again the example of Alpamayo R1

01:12:33.960 --> 01:12:37.520
from NVIDIA, which is an
autonomous driving reasoning

01:12:37.520 --> 01:12:38.920
VLA model.

01:12:38.920 --> 01:12:42.610
We are basically seeing how
for each of these stages,

01:12:42.610 --> 01:12:44.890
we have different algorithms
that are going on.

01:12:44.890 --> 01:12:46.850
So we will have,
at a high level,

01:12:46.850 --> 01:12:50.270
this is a model that uses
vision language models in order

01:12:50.270 --> 01:12:52.790
to compute actions for the car.

01:12:52.790 --> 01:12:55.490
So there is a general
pre-training phase that happens,

01:12:55.490 --> 01:12:57.490
and this is kind of
internet scale pre-training.

01:12:57.490 --> 01:12:59.230
So just supervised learning.

01:12:59.230 --> 01:13:02.630
Then we will have some
SFT or supervised fine

01:13:02.630 --> 01:13:05.390
tuning, which is basically
imitation learning.

01:13:05.390 --> 01:13:07.270
And then we'll
have a final stage

01:13:07.270 --> 01:13:08.870
where we use
reinforcement learning

01:13:08.870 --> 01:13:12.670
to either align the performance
with human understanding

01:13:12.670 --> 01:13:16.110
and human values but
also just to squeeze out

01:13:16.110 --> 01:13:17.830
the last bit of performance.

01:13:17.830 --> 01:13:19.630
And so I think it's
really important

01:13:19.630 --> 01:13:22.110
that you see these tools
as different elements

01:13:22.110 --> 01:13:25.050
that all together build
up for autonomous stacks.

01:13:29.030 --> 01:13:29.830
Great.

01:13:29.830 --> 01:13:30.810
Any questions?

01:13:33.630 --> 01:13:36.620
Then see you on Monday.

